(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqDual=[{pn:1,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A monochromatic light is incident on a metallic plate of work function \u03C6. An electron emitted normally to the plate from a point A with maximum kinetic energy enters a constant magnetic field perpendicular to its initial velocity. The electron moves along a curve and hits the plate again at a point B. The magnetic field exists throughout the path. Charge of electron e, mass m, Planck's constant h, speed of light c. The distance between A and B is",o:["\u221A(2m(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6))/eB","2\u221A(m(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6))/eB","\u221A(m(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6))/eB","\u221A(8m(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6))/eB"],c:3,s:`Kmax = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6.
In the field the path is a semicircle, so AB = 2r.
r = mv/(eB) = \u221A(2m\xB7Kmax)/(eB).
AB = 2\u221A(2m\xB7Kmax)/(eB) = \u221A(8m(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6))/eB.`},{pn:2,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The work function of a metal is 3 eV. The colour of the visible light that is required to cause emission of photoelectrons is",o:["Yellow","Blue","Green","Red"],c:1,s:`Threshold wavelength = 1240/3 \u2248 413 nm.
Light must have \u03BB \u2264 413 nm, i.e. the violet-blue end of the spectrum.
Of the options only blue is closest to this, so blue.`},{pn:3,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The work functions of cesium (Cs) and lithium (Li) metals are 1.9 eV and 2.5 eV, respectively. If we incident a light of wavelength 550 nm on these two metal surfaces, then photo-electric effect is possible for the case of",o:["Li only","Neither Cs nor Li","Both Cs and Li","Cs only"],c:3,s:`Photon energy = 1240/550 \u2248 2.25 eV.
2.25 > 1.9 (Cs) so Cs emits.
2.25 < 2.5 (Li) so Li does not.
Answer: Cs only.`},{pn:4,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A light source of wavelength \u03BB illuminates a metal surface and electrons are ejected with maximum kinetic energy of 2 eV. If the same surface is illuminated by a light source of wavelength \u03BB/2, then the maximum kinetic energy of ejected electrons will be (The work function of the metal is 1 eV)",o:["6 eV","3 eV","5 eV","2 eV"],c:2,s:`Photon energy at \u03BB: E = K + \u03C6 = 2 + 1 = 3 eV.
At \u03BB/2 the photon energy doubles: 6 eV.
K' = 6 \u2212 1 = 5 eV.`},{pn:5,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"In photoelectric effect an em-wave is incident on a metal surface and electrons are ejected from the surface. If the work function of the metal is 2.14 eV and stopping potential is 2 V, what is the wavelength of the em-wave? (Given hc = 1242 eV nm)",o:["400 nm","200 nm","600 nm","300 nm"],c:3,s:`Photon energy = \u03C6 + eV\u2080 = 2.14 + 2 = 4.14 eV.
\u03BB = 1242/4.14 = 300 nm.`},{pn:6,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:`In photoelectric effect, the stopping potential (V\u2080) v/s frequency (\u03BD) curve is plotted. (h is Planck's constant and \u03C6\u2080 is work function of metal)
(A) V\u2080 v/s \u03BD is linear.
(B) The slope of V\u2080 v/s \u03BD curve = \u03C6\u2080/h
(C) h constant is related to the slope of V\u2080 v/s \u03BD line.
(D) The value of electric charge of electron is not required to determine h using the V\u2080 v/s \u03BD curve.
(E) The work function can be estimated without knowing the value of h.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(D) and (E) only","(A), (B) and (C) only","(A), (C) and (E) only","(C) and (D) only"],c:2,s:`eV\u2080 = h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6, so V\u2080 = (h/e)\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6/e: linear (A true).
Slope = h/e, not \u03C6\u2080/h (B false); slope gives h but only if e is known (C true, D false).
The V\u2080-axis intercept is \u2212\u03C6/e, so \u03C6 = e \xD7 intercept without using h (E true).
Answer: A, C, E.`},{pn:7,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"In an experiment with photoelectric effect, the stopping potential,",o:["is (1/e) times the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons","decreases with increase in the intensity of the incident light","increases with increase in the wavelength of the incident light","increases with increase in the intensity of the incident light"],c:0,s:`Stopping potential satisfies eV\u2080 = Kmax, so V\u2080 = Kmax/e: (a) is true.
Kmax depends on frequency, not intensity: (b) and (d) are false.
V\u2080 = (hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6)/e falls as \u03BB grows: (c) is false.`},{pn:8,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"Which figure shows the correct variation of applied potential difference (V) with photoelectric current (i) at two different intensities of light (I\u2081 < I\u2082) of same wavelengths?",o:["Graph (a) of the figure","Graph (b) of the figure","Graph (c) of the figure","Graph (d) of the figure"],c:2,s:`Same wavelength and metal means the same Kmax, so the same stopping potential \u2212V\u2080 for both curves.
Saturation current is proportional to intensity, so the I\u2082 curve lies higher.
Only graph (c) has both curves starting at the same \u2212V\u2080 with I\u2082 above I\u2081.`,fig:{w:380,h:280,alt:"Four graphs (a) to (d) of photocurrent i against applied potential V for two intensities I1 less than I2: (a) curves start at different stopping potentials and cross; (b) curves start at different points, one at -V0 and one at zero; (c) both curves start at the same -V0 with I2 above I1; (d) both start at slightly different negative potentials with I2 above I1.",d:[["l",50,90,195,90,"a"],["l",110,102,110,10,"a"],["t",198,94,"V","s"],["t",114,12,"i","s"],["t",10,120,"(a)","s"],["p","M70 90 C78 56 100 42 155 42"],["t",158,45,"I\u2081","s"],["p","M95 90 C103 41 125 20 180 20"],["t",183,23,"I\u2082","s"],["l",240,90,385,90,"a"],["l",300,102,300,10,"a"],["t",388,94,"V","s"],["t",304,12,"i","s"],["t",200,120,"(b)","s"],["p","M245 90 C253 48 275 30 330 30"],["t",333,33,"I\u2081","s"],["p","M300 90 C308 37 330 15 385 15"],["t",388,18,"I\u2082","s"],["l",50,230,195,230,"a"],["l",110,242,110,150,"a"],["t",198,234,"V","s"],["t",114,152,"i","s"],["t",10,260,"(c)","s"],["p","M75 230 C83 198 105 185 160 185"],["t",163,188,"I\u2081","s"],["p","M75 230 C83 184 105 165 160 165"],["t",163,168,"I\u2082","s"],["l",240,230,385,230,"a"],["l",300,242,300,150,"a"],["t",388,234,"V","s"],["t",304,152,"i","s"],["t",200,260,"(d)","s"],["p","M270 230 C278 198 300 185 355 185"],["t",358,188,"I\u2081","s"],["p","M255 230 C263 188 285 170 340 170"],["t",343,173,"I\u2082","s"],["t",62,104,"-V\u2080","s"],["t",237,104,"-V\u2080","s"],["t",67,244,"-V\u2080","s"],["t",247,244,"-V\u2080","s"]]}},{pn:9,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"Which of the following statement is not true about stopping potential (V\u2080)?",o:["It depends on the nature of emitter material.","It increases with increase in intensity of the incident light.","It depends upon frequency of the incident light.","It is 1/e times the maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted."],c:1,s:`V\u2080 = (h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6)/e depends on material (\u03C6) and frequency, and equals Kmax/e.
It does not depend on intensity, so (b) is the untrue statement.`},{pn:10,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"In photoelectric experiment energy of 2.48 eV irradiates a photo sensitive material. The stopping potential was measured to be 0.5 V. Work function of the photo sensitive material is",o:["1.68 eV","2.48 eV","0.5 eV","1.98 eV"],c:3,s:`Kmax = eV\u2080 = 0.5 eV.
\u03C6 = E \u2212 Kmax = 2.48 \u2212 0.5 = 1.98 eV.`},{pn:11,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Which of the following phenomena does not explain by wave nature of light?
(A) reflection
(B) diffraction
(C) photoelectric effect
(D) interference
(E) polarization
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["A, C only","E only","B, D only","C only"],c:3,s:`Reflection, diffraction, interference and polarization are all explained by waves.
The photoelectric effect needs the photon (particle) picture.
Answer: C only.`},{pn:12,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"When UV light of wavelength 300 nm is incident on the metal surface having work function 2.13 eV, electron emission takes place. The stopping potential is (Given hc = 1240 eV nm)",o:["4.1 V","1.5 V","2 V","4 V"],c:2,s:`E = 1240/300 = 4.13 eV.
eV\u2080 = 4.13 \u2212 2.13 = 2.0 eV, so V\u2080 = 2 V.`},{pn:13,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"UV light of 4.13 eV is incident on a photosensitive metal surface having work function 3.13 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of ejected photoelectrons will be",o:["4.13 eV","1 eV","3.13 eV","7.26 eV"],c:1,s:"Kmax = 4.13 \u2212 3.13 = 1 eV."},{pn:14,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"Monochromatic light of frequency 6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz is produced by a laser. The power emitted is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 W. How many photons per second on an average, are emitted by the source? (Given h = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075","7 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075","9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078"],c:0,s:`Photon energy = h\u03BD = 6.63e-34 \xD7 6e14 = 3.98e-19 J.
N = P/h\u03BD = 2e-3/3.98e-19 \u2248 5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 per second.`},{pn:15,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"A convex lens of focal length 40 cm forms an image of an extended source of light on a photoelectric cell. A current I is produced. The lens is replaced by another convex lens having the same diameter but focal length 20 cm. The photoelectric current now is",o:["2I","4I","I/2","I"],c:3,s:`The lens collects light through the same aperture, so the same total power falls on the image whatever its size.
Same power means same number of photons per second, so the current stays I.`},{pn:16,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The threshold frequency of a metal with work function 6.63 eV is",o:["16 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 Hz","1.6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 Hz","16 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 Hz","1.6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 Hz"],c:3,s:"\u03BD\u2080 = \u03C6/h = (6.63 \xD7 1.6e-19)/(6.63e-34) = 1.6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 Hz."},{pn:17,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"Two sources of light emit with a power of 200 W. The ratio of number of photons of visible light emitted by each source having wavelengths 300 nm and 500 nm respectively, will be",o:["1 : 5","5 : 3","1 : 3","3 : 5"],c:3,s:`N = P/(hc/\u03BB) = P\u03BB/hc, so N \u221D \u03BB at equal power.
N\u2081 : N\u2082 = 300 : 500 = 3 : 5.`},{pn:18,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The work function of a substance is 3.0 eV. The longest wavelength of light that can cause the emission of photoelectrons from this substance is approximately",o:["215 nm","200 nm","414 nm","400 nm"],c:2,s:"\u03BBmax = hc/\u03C6 = 1240/3.0 \u2248 413-414 nm."},{pn:19,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"For the photoelectric effect, the maximum kinetic energy (Ek) of the photoelectrons is plotted against the frequency (\u03BD) of the incident photons as shown in figure. The slope of the graph gives",o:["ratio of Planck's constant to electric charge","charge of electron","Planck's constant","work function of the metal"],c:2,s:`Kmax = h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6, a straight line in \u03BD.
Its slope is h, Planck's constant.`,fig:{w:230,h:170,alt:"Graph of maximum kinetic energy Ek on the vertical axis against frequency \u03BD on the horizontal axis: a straight line rising from a point on the \u03BD axis, making angle \u03B8 with it.",d:[["l",40,140,215,140,"a"],["l",40,140,40,15,"a"],["t",30,22,"E\u2096","e"],["t",208,156,"\u03BD"],["t",34,154,"O"],["l",95,140,175,40,"B"],["p","M118 140 A23 23 0 0 0 113 124"],["t",122,134,"\u03B8","s"]]}},{pn:20,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"When a metal surface is illuminated by light of wavelength \u03BB, the stopping potential is 8 V. When the same surface is illuminated by light of wavelength 3\u03BB, stopping potential is 2 V. The threshold wavelength for this surface is",o:["4.5\u03BB","3\u03BB","5\u03BB","9\u03BB"],c:3,s:`hc/\u03BB = \u03C6 + 8e and hc/3\u03BB = \u03C6 + 2e (energies in eV).
Subtract: (2/3)(hc/\u03BB) = 6 eV, so hc/\u03BB = 9 eV.
Then \u03C6 = 9 \u2212 8 = 1 eV, i.e. \u03C6 = (1/9)(hc/\u03BB).
\u03BB\u2080 = hc/\u03C6 = 9\u03BB.`},{pn:21,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"In a photoelectric effect experiment a light of frequency 1.5 times the threshold frequency is made to fall on the surface of photosensitive material. Now if the frequency is halved and intensity is doubled, the number of photo electrons emitted will be",o:["halved","quadrupled","Zero","doubled"],c:2,s:`New frequency = 0.75 \u03BD\u2080, which is below the threshold \u03BD\u2080.
No photoemission occurs however high the intensity, so the number is zero.`},{pn:22,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"The work functions of Aluminium and Gold are 4.1 eV and 5.1 eV respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for Gold to that of Aluminium is",o:["1.5","1","1.24","2"],c:1,s:`V\u2080 = (h/e)\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6/e, so the slope is h/e for every metal.
The ratio is 1.`},{pn:23,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`In photoelectric effect
A. The photocurrent is proportional to the intensity of the incident radiation.
B. Maximum Kinetic energy with which photoelectrons are emitted depends on the intensity of incident light.
C. Max. K.E. with which photoelectrons are emitted depends on the frequency of incident light.
D. The emission of photoelectrons require a minimum threshold intensity of incident radiation.
E. Max K.E. of the photoelectrons is independent of the frequency of the incident light.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["B and C only","A and E only","A and C only","A and B only"],c:2,s:`A true (saturation current grows with intensity).
B false: Kmax does not depend on intensity.
C true: Kmax = h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6.
D false: the requirement is a threshold frequency, not intensity.
E false.
Answer: A and C only.`},{pn:24,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"The variation of stopping potential (V\u2080) as a function of the frequency (\u03BD) of the incident light for a metal is shown in figure. The work function of the surface is",o:["18.6 eV","2.07 eV","1.36 eV","2.98 eV"],c:1,s:`The line meets the frequency axis at \u03BD\u2080 = 5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz.
\u03C6 = h\u03BD\u2080 = (6.63e-34 \xD7 5e14)/1.6e-19 \u2248 2.07 eV.`,fig:{w:270,h:185,alt:"Graph of stopping potential V\u2080 in volt (vertical, marks 1 to 3) against frequency \u03BD in units of 10\xB9\u2074 Hz (horizontal, marks 0 to 10). A straight line starts on the \u03BD axis at 5 and rises to about 2.1 V at 10.",d:[["l",40,150,255,150,"a"],["l",40,150,40,15,"a"],["l",60,146,60,154],["l",80,146,80,154],["l",100,146,100,154],["l",120,146,120,154],["l",140,146,140,154],["l",160,146,160,154],["l",180,146,180,154],["l",200,146,200,154],["l",220,146,220,154],["l",240,146,240,154],["t",60,166,"1","m","s"],["t",100,166,"3","m","s"],["t",140,166,"5","m","s"],["t",180,166,"7","m","s"],["t",220,166,"9","m","s"],["t",40,180,"\u03BD (\xD710\xB9\u2074 Hz)","s"],["l",36,115,44,115],["l",36,80,44,80],["l",36,45,44,45],["t",30,119,"1","e","s"],["t",30,84,"2","e","s"],["t",30,49,"3","e","s"],["t",48,14,"V\u2080 (volt)","s"],["l",140,150,240,78,"B"]]}},{pn:25,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength \u03BB, the stopping potential is V\u2080. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength 2\u03BB, the stopping potential becomes V\u2080/4. The threshold wavelength for this metallic surface will be",o:["\u03BB/4","4\u03BB","3\u03BB/2","3\u03BB"],c:3,s:`hc/\u03BB = \u03C6 + eV\u2080 and hc/2\u03BB = \u03C6 + eV\u2080/4.
Subtract: hc/2\u03BB = 3eV\u2080/4, so eV\u2080 = 2hc/3\u03BB.
\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB \u2212 2hc/3\u03BB = hc/3\u03BB.
\u03BB\u2080 = hc/\u03C6 = 3\u03BB.`},{pn:26,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The difference between threshold wavelengths for two metal surfaces A and B having work function \u03C6A = 9 eV and \u03C6B = 4.5 eV in nm is (Given, hc = 1242 eV nm)",o:["540","264","276","138"],c:3,s:`\u03BBA = 1242/9 = 138 nm; \u03BBB = 1242/4.5 = 276 nm.
Difference = 138 nm.`},{pn:27,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The threshold frequency of a metal is f\u2080. When the light of frequency 2f\u2080 is incident on the metal plate, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons is v\u2081. When the frequency of incident radiation is increased to 5f\u2080, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons emitted is v\u2082. The ratio of v\u2081 to v\u2082 is",o:["v\u2081/v\u2082 = 1/8","v\u2081/v\u2082 = 1/4","v\u2081/v\u2082 = 1/16","v\u2081/v\u2082 = 1/2"],c:3,s:`\xBDmv\xB2 = h(f \u2212 f\u2080).
For 2f\u2080: \xBDmv\u2081\xB2 = hf\u2080. For 5f\u2080: \xBDmv\u2082\xB2 = 4hf\u2080.
v\u2081\xB2/v\u2082\xB2 = 1/4, so v\u2081/v\u2082 = 1/2.`},{pn:28,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`From the photoelectric effect experiment, following are observations made. Identify which of these are correct.
A. The stopping potential depends only on the work function of the metal.
B. The saturation current increases as the intensity of incident light increases.
C. The maximum kinetic energy of a photo electron depends on the intensity of the incident light.
D. Photoelectric effect can be explained using wave theory of light
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A, B, D only","B only","A, C, D only","B, C only"],c:1,s:`A false: V\u2080 also depends on frequency.
B true.
C false: Kmax is independent of intensity.
D false: wave theory cannot explain it.
Answer: B only.`},{pn:29,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"The threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a material is 5500 \xC5. Photoelectrons will be emitted, when this material is illuminated with monochromatic radiation from a",o:["75 W infra-red lamp","10 W infra-red lamp","75 W ultra-violet lamp","10 W ultra-violet lamp"],c:[2,3],s:`Emission needs \u03BB < 5500 \xC5 (frequency above threshold). Infrared has longer wavelength, so no emission whatever the power.
Ultraviolet has shorter wavelength, so emission occurs for both the 75 W and the 10 W lamp (power only changes the number of electrons).
Note: the printed key is not given; both (c) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:30,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"If a source of electromagnetic radiation having power 15 kW produces 10\xB9\u2076 photons per second, the radiation belongs to a part of spectrum is (Take Planck constant h = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["Micro waves","Gamma rays","Radio wave","Ultraviolet rays"],c:1,s:`Energy per photon = 15000/10\xB9\u2076 = 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.
\u03BD = E/h = 1.5e-12/6e-34 = 2.5 \xD7 10\xB2\xB9 Hz.
This is in the gamma-ray region.`},{pn:31,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"Two metals A and B are exposed to radiation of wavelength 350 nm. The work functions of A and B are 4.8 eV and 2.2 eV. Choose the correct option.",o:["Both metals A and B will emit photo-electrons.","Metal B will not emit photo-electrons.","Both metals A and B will not emit photo-electrons.","Metal A will not emit photo-electrons."],c:3,s:`Photon energy = 1240/350 = 3.54 eV.
A: 3.54 < 4.8 eV, no emission.
B: 3.54 > 2.2 eV, emits.
So only A does not emit.`},{pn:32,y:"JEE Main 2023",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"A metal exposed to light of wavelength 800 nm emits photoelectrons with a certain kinetic energy. The maximum kinetic energy of the photo-electron doubles when light of wavelength 500 nm is used. The work function of the metal is (Take hc = 1230 eV nm).",o:["1.537 eV","2.46 eV","0.615 eV","1.23 eV"],c:2,s:`E1 = 1230/800 = 1.5375 eV, E2 = 1230/500 = 2.46 eV.
K2 = 2K1: 2.46 \u2212 \u03C6 = 2(1.5375 \u2212 \u03C6).
\u03C6 = 3.075 \u2212 2.46 = 0.615 eV.`},{pn:33,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A parallel beam of light of wavelength 900 nm and intensity 100 W m\u207B\xB2 is incident on a surface perpendicular to the beam. The number of photons crossing 1 cm\xB2 area perpendicular to the beam in one second is",o:["3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2077","4.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2070"],c:1,s:`Energy per second through 1 cm\xB2 = 100 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 10\u207B\xB2 W.
Photon energy = hc/\u03BB = 6.63\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074\xD73\xD710\u2078/900\xD710\u207B\u2079 = 2.21\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
N = 10\u207B\xB2/2.21\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2248 4.5\xD710\xB9\u2076.`},{pn:34,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`With reference to the observations in photo-electric effect, identify the correct statements from below:
(A) The square of maximum velocity of photoelectrons varies linearly with frequency of incident light.
(B) The value of saturation current increases on moving the source of light away from the metal surface.
(C) The maximum kinetic energy of photo-electrons decreases on decreasing the power of LED (light emitting diode) source of light.
(D) The immediate emission of photo-electrons out of metal surface cannot be explained by particle nature of light/electromagnetic waves.
(E) Existence of threshold wavelength cannot be explained by wave nature of light/electromagnetic waves.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(A) and (B) only","(A) and (E) only","(C) and (E) only","(D) and (E) only"],c:1,s:`(A) true: v\xB2 = 2h(f \u2212 f\u2080)/m is linear in f.
(B) false: moving the source away lowers intensity, so saturation current falls.
(C) false: Kmax does not depend on power.
(D) false: instant emission is explained by the particle nature; the wave nature cannot explain it.
(E) true: the wave theory predicts no threshold.
So (A) and (E).`},{pn:35,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Two streams of photons, possessing energies equal to five and ten times the work function of a metal, are incident on the metal surface successively. The ratio of maximum velocities of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases, respectively, will be",o:["1 : 2","1 : 3","2 : 3","3 : 2"],c:2,s:`K = E \u2212 \u03C6: first K = 4\u03C6, second K = 9\u03C6.
v \u221D \u221AK, so v1 : v2 = \u221A4 : \u221A9 = 2 : 3.`},{pn:36,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"The kinetic energy of the emitted electron is E when the light incident on the metal has wavelength \u03BB. To double the kinetic energy, the incident light must have wavelength",o:["hc / (E\u03BB \u2212 hc)","hc\u03BB / (E\u03BB + hc)","h\u03BB / (E\u03BB + hc)","hc\u03BB / (E\u03BB \u2212 hc)"],c:1,s:`\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB \u2212 E.
For 2E: hc/\u03BB' = 2E + \u03C6 = E + hc/\u03BB = (E\u03BB + hc)/\u03BB.
\u03BB' = hc\u03BB / (E\u03BB + hc).`},{pn:37,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Light of two different frequencies, whose photons have energies 3.8 eV and 1.4 eV respectively, illuminates a metallic surface of work function 0.6 eV successively. The ratio of maximum speeds of the emitted electrons for the two frequencies respectively will be",o:["1 : 1","2 : 1","4 : 1","1 : 4"],c:1,s:`K1 = 3.8 \u2212 0.6 = 3.2 eV, K2 = 1.4 \u2212 0.6 = 0.8 eV.
K1/K2 = 4, so v1/v2 = \u221A4 = 2 : 1.`},{pn:38,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A metal surface is illuminated by radiation of wavelength 4500 \xC5. The ejected photo-electron enters a constant magnetic field of 2 mT making an angle of 90\xB0 with the field. If it starts revolving in a circular path of radius 2 mm, the work function of the metal is approximately",o:["1.36 eV","1.69 eV","2.78 eV","2.23 eV"],c:0,s:`K = (eBr)\xB2/2m = (1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2\xD710\u207B\xB3)\xB2 / (2 \xD7 9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9) = 2.25\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J = 1.41 eV.
Photon energy = 1240/450 = 2.76 eV.
\u03C6 = 2.76 \u2212 1.41 \u2248 1.36 eV.`},{pn:39,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Let K\u2081 and K\u2082 be the maximum kinetic energies of photo-electrons emitted when two monochromatic beams of wavelengths \u03BB\u2081 and \u03BB\u2082 respectively are incident on a metallic surface. If \u03BB\u2081 = 3\u03BB\u2082 then",o:["K\u2081 > K\u2082/3","K\u2081 < K\u2082/3","K\u2081 = K\u2082/3","K\u2082 = K\u2081/3"],c:1,s:`K\u2082 = hc/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6 = 3(hc/\u03BB\u2081) \u2212 \u03C6 = 3(K\u2081 + \u03C6) \u2212 \u03C6 = 3K\u2081 + 2\u03C6.
Since \u03C6 > 0, K\u2082 > 3K\u2081, i.e. K\u2081 < K\u2082/3.`},{pn:40,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The electric field at a point associated with a light wave is E = 200[sin(6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075)t + sin(9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075)t] V m\u207B\xB9 (Given h = 4.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 eV s). If this light falls on a metal surface having a work function of 2.50 eV, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons will be",o:["1.90 eV","3.27 eV","3.60 eV","3.42 eV"],c:3,s:`The larger angular frequency is \u03C9 = 9\xD710\xB9\u2075 rad/s, so f = \u03C9/2\u03C0 = 1.43\xD710\xB9\u2075 Hz.
E = hf = 4.14\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 \xD7 1.43\xD710\xB9\u2075 \u2248 5.93 eV.
K max = 5.93 \u2212 2.50 \u2248 3.42 eV.`},{pn:41,y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When light of a given wavelength is incident on a metallic surface, the minimum potential needed to stop the emitted photoelectrons is 6.0 V. This potential drops to 0.6 V if another source with wavelength four times that of the first one and intensity half of the first one is used. What are the wavelength of the first source and the work function of the metal, respectively? [Take hc/e = 1.24 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J m C\u207B\xB9.]",o:["1.72 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m, 1.20 eV","1.72 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m, 5.60 eV","3.78 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m, 5.60 eV","3.78 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m, 1.20 eV"],c:0,s:`Intensity does not change the stopping potential.
hc/(e\u03BB) \u2212 \u03C6/e = 6 and hc/(4e\u03BB) \u2212 \u03C6/e = 0.6.
Subtract: (3/4)(hc/e\u03BB) = 5.4, so hc/e\u03BB = 7.2 V.
\u03BB = 1.24\xD710\u207B\u2076/7.2 = 1.72\xD710\u207B\u2077 m; \u03C6 = 7.2 \u2212 6 = 1.2 eV.`},{pn:42,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"In a photoelectric experiment, ultraviolet light of wavelength 280 nm is used with a lithium cathode having work function \u03C6 = 2.5 eV. If the wavelength of incident light is switched to 400 nm, find the change in the stopping potential. (h = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, c = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m s\u207B\xB9)",o:["1.1 V","0.6 V","1.3 V","1.9 V"],c:2,s:`hc/e = 1.243\xD710\u207B\u2076 V m.
At 280 nm: 4.44 eV, V\u2081 = 4.44 \u2212 2.5 = 1.94 V.
At 400 nm: 3.11 eV, V\u2082 = 0.61 V.
Change \u2248 1.3 V.`},{pn:43,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"In a photoelectric experiment, increasing the intensity of incident light",o:["increases the number of photons incident and also increases the K.E. of the ejected electrons.","increases the frequency of photons incident and increases the K.E. of the ejected electrons.","increases the number of photons incident and the K.E. of the ejected electrons remains unchanged.","increases the frequency of photons incident and the K.E. of the ejected electrons remains unchanged."],c:2,s:`Intensity means more photons per second; each photon's energy hf is unchanged.
So K max = hf \u2212 \u03C6 stays the same and only the photocurrent rises.`},{pn:44,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"A monochromatic neon lamp with wavelength 670.5 nm illuminates a photo-sensitive material which has a stopping voltage of 0.48 V. What will be the stopping voltage if the source light is changed to another source of wavelength 476.6 nm?",o:["0.24 V","1.5 V","1.25 V","0.96 V"],c:2,s:`Using hc = 1240 eV nm: E1 = 1.849 eV, so \u03C6 = 1.849 \u2212 0.48 = 1.369 eV.
E2 = 1240/476.6 = 2.602 eV.
V\u2082 = 2.602 \u2212 1.369 \u2248 1.23 V, nearest 1.25 V.`},{pn:45,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When radiation of wavelength \u03BB is incident on a metallic surface, the stopping potential of ejected photoelectrons is 4.8 V. If the same surface is illuminated by radiation of double the previous wavelength, the stopping potential becomes 1.6 V. The threshold wavelength of the metal is",o:["4\u03BB","8\u03BB","2\u03BB","6\u03BB"],c:0,s:`hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = 4.8 eV and hc/2\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = 1.6 eV.
Subtract: hc/2\u03BB = 3.2 eV, so hc/\u03BB = 6.4 eV and \u03C6 = 1.6 eV.
\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB\u2080 = (6.4 eV)(\u03BB/\u03BB\u2080) = 1.6 eV, so \u03BB\u2080 = 4\u03BB.`},{pn:46,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"The stopping potential in the context of the photoelectric effect depends on the following property of incident electromagnetic radiation",o:["phase","frequency","intensity","amplitude"],c:1,s:"eV\u2080 = hf \u2212 \u03C6, so V\u2080 depends only on frequency (for a given metal), not on intensity or amplitude."},{pn:47,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Two identical photocathodes receive light of frequencies f\u2081 and f\u2082 respectively. If the velocities of the photo-electrons coming out are v\u2081 and v\u2082 respectively, then",o:["v\u2081 \u2212 v\u2082 = [2h(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082)/m]^(1/2)","v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2 = 2h(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082)/m","v\u2081 + v\u2082 = [2h(f\u2081 + f\u2082)/m]^(1/2)","v\u2081\xB2 + v\u2082\xB2 = 2h(f\u2081 + f\u2082)/m"],c:1,s:`\xBDmv\u2081\xB2 = hf\u2081 \u2212 \u03C6 and \xBDmv\u2082\xB2 = hf\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6.
Subtract (same \u03C6): \xBDm(v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2) = h(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082).
v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2 = 2h(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082)/m.`},{pn:48,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"The stopping potential for electrons emitted from a photosensitive surface illuminated by light of wavelength 491 nm is 0.710 V. When the incident wavelength is changed to a new value, the stopping potential is 1.43 V. The new wavelength is",o:["382 nm","309 nm","329 nm","400 nm"],c:0,s:`E1 = 1240/491 = 2.526 eV, so \u03C6 = 2.526 \u2212 0.710 = 1.816 eV.
New photon energy = 1.43 + 1.816 = 3.246 eV.
\u03BB = 1240/3.246 \u2248 382 nm.`},{pn:49,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When the wavelength of radiation falling on a metal is changed from 500 nm to 200 nm, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons becomes three times larger. The work function of the metal is close to",o:["0.81 eV","1.02 eV","0.52 eV","0.61 eV"],c:3,s:`E1 = 2.48 eV, E2 = 6.2 eV.
6.2 \u2212 \u03C6 = 3(2.48 \u2212 \u03C6) = 7.44 \u2212 3\u03C6.
2\u03C6 = 1.24, so \u03C6 \u2248 0.62 eV (0.61 eV).`},{pn:50,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"Two sources of light emit X-rays of wavelength 1 nm and visible light of wavelength 500 nm, respectively. Both the sources emit light of the same power 200 W. The ratio of the number density of photons of X-rays to the number density of photons of the visible light of the given wavelengths is",o:["1/500","500","1/250","250"],c:0,s:`Number density n = P/(E\xB7c\xB7A) with E = hc/\u03BB, so n \u221D \u03BB at equal power.
Ratio = 1 nm / 500 nm = 1/500.`},{pn:51,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"Given figure shows few data points in a photoelectric effect experiment for a certain metal. The minimum energy for ejection of electrons from its surface is (Planck's constant h = 6.62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["2.27 eV","2.10 eV","2.59 eV","1.93 eV"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key says (b) 2.10 eV; working gives (a) 2.27 eV.
Point B (5.5, 0) lies on the frequency axis, so the threshold frequency is 5.5\xD710\xB9\u2074 Hz.
\u03C6 = hf\u2080 = 6.62\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 5.5\xD710\xB9\u2074 = 3.64\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
\u03C6 = 3.64\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 / 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2248 2.27 eV.`,fig:{w:240,h:170,alt:"Graph of stopping potential against frequency in units of 10^14 Hz: a straight line rising to the right; point A near frequency 5, point B at (5.5, 0) on the frequency axis, point C at (6, V) higher on the line.",d:[["l",30,140,220,140,"a"],["l",30,150,30,10,"a"],["t",224,144,"f (10\xB9\u2074 Hz)","s"],["t",6,14,"Vstop","s"],["l",60,150,170,30,"B"],["c",80,128,3,"F"],["c",100,106,3,"F"],["c",150,52,3,"F"],["t",66,126,"A","s"],["t",104,120,"B (5.5, 0)","s"],["t",154,48,"C (6, V)","s"],["l",150,52,30,52,"d"],["t",34,56,"V","s"],["t",78,156,"5","s"]]}},{pn:52,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"In a photoelectric effect experiment, the graph of stopping potential V versus reciprocal of wavelength obtained is shown in the figure. As the intensity of incident radiation is increased",o:["straight line shifts to left","straight line shifts to right","graph does not change","slope of the straight line get more steep."],c:2,s:`V = (hc/e)(1/\u03BB) \u2212 \u03C6/e.
Slope hc/e and intercept \u03C6/e do not involve intensity.
So the graph does not change.`,fig:{w:220,h:150,alt:"Graph of stopping potential V against 1/\u03BB: a straight line rising to the right from the horizontal axis with angle \u03B8 marked at its foot.",d:[["l",30,120,200,120,"a"],["l",30,130,30,10,"a"],["t",204,124,"1/\u03BB","s"],["t",16,14,"V","s"],["l",60,120,170,25,"B"],["p","M85 120 A25 25 0 0 0 80 100"],["t",90,114,"\u03B8","s"]]}},{pn:53,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"Radiation, with wavelength 6561 \xC5, falls on a metal surface to produce photoelectrons. The electrons are made to enter a uniform magnetic field of 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T. If the radius of the largest circular path followed by the electrons is 10 mm, the work function of the metal is close to",o:["0.8 eV","1.1 eV","1.8 eV","1.6 eV"],c:1,s:`K max = (eBr)\xB2/2m = (1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 3\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)\xB2 / (2 \xD7 9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9) = 1.27\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J = 0.79 eV.
Photon energy = 12375/6561 = 1.89 eV.
\u03C6 = 1.89 \u2212 0.79 \u2248 1.1 eV.`},{pn:54,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The electric field of a light wave is given as E = 10\u207B\xB3 cos(2\u03C0x/(5\xD710\u207B\u2077) \u2212 2\u03C0 \xD7 6\xD710\xB9\u2074 t) x\u0302 N/C. This light falls on a metal plate of work function 2 eV. The stopping potential of the photo electrons is [Given E (in eV) = 12375/\u03BB (in \xC5)]",o:["0.48 V","0.72 V","2.48 V","2.0 V"],c:0,s:`2\u03C0/\u03BB = 2\u03C0/(5\xD710\u207B\u2077 m), so \u03BB = 5\xD710\u207B\u2077 m = 5000 \xC5.
E = 12375/5000 = 2.475 eV.
eV\u2080 = 2.475 \u2212 2 = 0.475, so V\u2080 \u2248 0.48 V.`},{pn:55,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"In a photoelectric effect experiment the threshold wavelength of light is 380 nm. If the wavelength of incident light is 260 nm, the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons will be [Given E (in eV) = 1237/\u03BB (in nm)]",o:["15.1 eV","4.5 eV","1.5 eV","3.0 eV"],c:2,s:`\u03C6 = 1237/380 = 3.26 eV.
E = 1237/260 = 4.76 eV.
K max = 4.76 \u2212 3.26 = 1.5 eV.`},{pn:56,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A 2 mW laser operates at a wavelength of 500 nm. The number of photons that will be emitted per second is [Given: h = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, c = 3.0 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s]",o:["2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","1 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075"],c:3,s:`Photon energy = hc/\u03BB = 6.6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 3\xD710\u2078 / 5\xD710\u207B\u2077 = 3.96\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
N = 2\xD710\u207B\xB3 / 3.96\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2248 5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 per second.`},{pn:57,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"The stopping potential V\u2080 (in volt) as a function of frequency \u03BD for a sodium emitter is shown in the figure (a straight line cutting the frequency axis at 4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz; frequency axis marked 2 to 10 in units of 10\xB9\u2074 Hz, potential axis up to 3.0 V). The work function of sodium, from the data plotted in the figure, will be (Given: h = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",o:["1.95 eV","1.66 eV","1.82 eV","2.12 eV"],c:1,s:`The line meets the frequency axis at the threshold \u03BD\u2080 = 4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz.
\u03C6 = h\u03BD\u2080/e = 6.63\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 4\xD710\xB9\u2074 / 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 1.66 eV.`,fig:{w:270,h:190,alt:"Stopping potential against frequency for sodium: a straight line rising from the frequency axis near 4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz to about 2.5 V at 10 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz",d:[["l",40,160,250,160,"a"],["l",40,160,40,15,"a"],["t",255,164,"\u03BD (10\xB9\u2074 Hz)","s"],["t",46,18,"V\u2080 (V)","s"],["l",120,160,240,60],["l",37,120,43,120],["l",37,80,43,80],["l",37,40,43,40],["t",22,124,"1.0","s"],["t",22,84,"2.0","s"],["t",22,44,"3.0","s"],["t",80,174,"2","ms"],["t",120,174,"4","ms"],["t",160,174,"6","ms"],["t",200,174,"8","ms"],["t",240,174,"10","ms"]]}},{pn:58,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Surface of certain metal is first illuminated with light of wavelength \u03BB\u2081 = 350 nm and then, by light of wavelength \u03BB\u2082 = 540 nm. It is found that the maximum speed of the photo electrons in the two cases differ by a factor of 2. The work function of the metal (in eV) is close to (Energy of photon = 1240/\u03BB (in nm) eV)",o:["5.6","2.5","1.4","1.8"],c:3,s:`E\u2081 = 1240/350 = 3.54 eV, E\u2082 = 1240/540 = 2.30 eV.
v\u2081 = 2v\u2082, so K\u2081 = 4K\u2082: 3.54 \u2212 \u03C6 = 4(2.30 \u2212 \u03C6).
3\u03C6 = 9.19 \u2212 3.54 = 5.65, so \u03C6 \u2248 1.88 eV, close to 1.8 eV.`},{pn:59,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The magnetic field associated with a light wave is given, at the origin, by B = B\u2080[sin(3.14 \xD7 10\u2077)ct + sin(6.28 \xD7 10\u2077)ct]. If this light falls on a silver plate having a work function of 4.7 eV, what will be the maximum kinetic energy of the photo electrons? (c = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m s\u207B\xB9, h = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["12.5 eV","8.52 eV","6.82 eV","7.72 eV"],c:3,s:`Larger angular frequency \u03C9 = 6.28\xD710\u2077 \xD7 c, so f = \u03C9/2\u03C0 = 10\u2077 \xD7 3\xD710\u2078 = 3\xD710\xB9\u2075 Hz.
E = hf = 6.6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 3\xD710\xB9\u2075 = 1.98\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2078 J = 12.4 eV.
K max = 12.4 \u2212 4.7 \u2248 7.7 eV (7.72 eV).`},{pn:60,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A metal plate of area 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 is illuminated by radiation of intensity 16 mW/m\xB2. The work function of the metal is 5 eV. The energy of the incident photons is 10 eV and only 10% of it produces photo electrons. The number of emitted photo electrons per second and their maximum energy, respectively, will be [1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J]",o:["10\xB9\u2070 and 5 eV","10\xB9\xB2 and 5 eV","10\xB9\xB9 and 5 eV","10\xB9\u2074 and 10 eV"],c:2,s:`Power = 16\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 1.6\xD710\u207B\u2076 W.
Photons/s = 1.6\xD710\u207B\u2076 / (10 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 10\xB9\xB2.
10% eject electrons: 10\xB9\xB9 per second.
K max = 10 \u2212 5 = 5 eV.`},{pn:61,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"In a photoelectric experiment, the wavelength of the light incident on a metal is changed from 300 nm to 400 nm. The decrease in the stopping potential is close to (hc/e = 1240 nm\xB7V)",o:["2.0 V","0.5 V","1.0 V","1.5 V"],c:2,s:`eV\u2080 = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6, so the \u03C6 terms cancel.
\u0394V\u2080 = 1240(1/300 \u2212 1/400) = 1240 \xD7 100/120000 = 1.03 V
So the decrease is about 1.0 V.`},{pn:62,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"In a Franck\u2013Hertz experiment, an electron of energy 5.6 eV passes through mercury vapour and emerges with an energy 0.7 eV. The minimum wavelength of photons emitted by mercury atoms is close to",o:["220 nm","1700 nm","250 nm","2020 nm"],c:2,s:`Energy given to the atom = 5.6 \u2212 0.7 = 4.9 eV.
The largest photon energy is 4.9 eV, so the smallest wavelength is \u03BB = 1240/4.9 \u2248 253 nm \u2248 250 nm.`},{pn:63,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When a certain photosensitive surface is illuminated with monochromatic light of frequency \u03C5, the stopping potential for the photocurrent is \u2212V\u2080/2. When the surface is illuminated by monochromatic light of frequency \u03C5/2, the stopping potential is \u2212V\u2080. The threshold frequency for photoelectric emission is",o:["4\u03C5/3","2\u03C5","5\u03C5/3","3\u03C5/2"],c:3,s:`Note: the printed key says d (3\u03C5/2); as printed, the data cannot be satisfied (a lower frequency giving the larger stopping potential), so the key is kept but could not be verified by solving.
Einstein: h\u03C5 \u2212 h\u03C5\u2080 = eV_s for each light. Subtracting the two equations eliminates h\u03C5\u2080 and fixes eV\u2080 in terms of h\u03C5; the threshold then follows from either equation.`},{pn:64,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The maximum velocity of the photoelectrons emitted from the surface is v when light of frequency \u03BD falls on a metal surface. If the incident frequency is increased to 3\u03BD, the maximum velocity of the ejected photoelectrons will be",o:["more than \u221A3 v","less than \u221A3 v","v","equal to \u221A3 v"],c:0,s:`\xBDmv\xB2 = h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6.
At 3\u03BD: \xBDmv'\xB2 = 3h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6 = 3(h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6) + 2\u03C6.
So \xBDmv'\xB2 > 3\xB7\xBDmv\xB2, giving v' > \u221A3 v.`},{pn:65,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A Laser light of wavelength 660 nm is used to weld Retina detachment. If a Laser pulse of width 60 ms and power 0.5 kW is used, the approximate number of photons in the pulse are [Take Planck's constant h = 6.62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s]",o:["10\xB9\u2079","10\xB2\xB2","10\xB9\u2078","10\xB2\u2070"],c:3,s:`Energy of pulse = 500 \xD7 0.06 = 30 J.
Photon energy = hc/\u03BB = 6.62\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074\xD73\xD710\u2078/660\xD710\u207B\u2079 = 3.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
N = 30/3.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 10\xB2\u2070.`},{pn:66,y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"A photoelectric material having work function \u03C6\u2080 is illuminated with light of wavelength \u03BB (\u03BB < hc/\u03C6\u2080). The fastest photoelectron has a de Broglie wavelength \u03BB_d. A change in wavelength of the incident light by \u0394\u03BB results in a change \u0394\u03BB_d in \u03BB_d. Then the ratio \u0394\u03BB_d/\u0394\u03BB is proportional to",o:["\u03BB_d\xB3/\u03BB","\u03BB_d\xB3/\u03BB\xB2","\u03BB_d\xB2/\u03BB\xB2","\u03BB_d/\u03BB"],c:1,s:`Fastest electron: K = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6\u2080 and \u03BB_d = h/\u221A(2mK), so \u03BB_d\xB2 = h\xB2/(2mK).
Differentiate: 2\u03BB_d \u0394\u03BB_d = \u2212(h\xB2/2mK\xB2)\u0394K, and \u0394K = \u2212(hc/\u03BB\xB2)\u0394\u03BB.
Use 1/K = 2m\u03BB_d\xB2/h\xB2: this gives 2\u03BB_d\u0394\u03BB_d \u221D \u03BB_d\u2074 \u0394\u03BB/\u03BB\xB2.
Hence \u0394\u03BB_d/\u0394\u03BB \u221D \u03BB_d\xB3/\u03BB\xB2.`},{pn:67,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Radiation of wavelength \u03BB is incident on a photocell. The fastest emitted electron has speed v. If the wavelength is changed to 3\u03BB/4, the speed of the fastest emitted electron will be",o:["> v(4/3)^(1/2)","< v(4/3)^(1/2)","= v(4/3)^(1/2)","= v(3/4)^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`K = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = \xBDmv\xB2.
At 3\u03BB/4: K\u2032 = (4/3)hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = (4/3)(K + \u03C6) \u2212 \u03C6 = (4/3)K + \u03C6/3.
So K\u2032 > (4/3)K and v\u2032 > v\u221A(4/3).`},{pn:68,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When photons of wavelength \u03BB\u2081 are incident on an isolated sphere, the corresponding stopping potential is found to be V. When photons of wavelength \u03BB\u2082 are used, the corresponding stopping potential was thrice that of the above value. If light of wavelength \u03BB\u2083 is used, then find the stopping potential for this case",o:["(hc/e)[1/\u03BB\u2083 + 1/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2081]","(hc/e)[1/\u03BB\u2083 + 1/2\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2081]","(hc/e)[1/\u03BB\u2083 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2081]","(hc/e)[1/\u03BB\u2083 + 1/2\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 3/2\u03BB\u2081]"],c:3,s:`eV = hc/\u03BB\u2081 \u2212 \u03C6 and 3eV = hc/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6.
Subtract: 2eV = hc(1/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2081), so eV = (hc/2)(1/\u03BB\u2082 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2081).
\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB\u2081 \u2212 eV = hc(3/2\u03BB\u2081 \u2212 1/2\u03BB\u2082).
V\u2083 = (hc/e)[1/\u03BB\u2083 \u2212 3/2\u03BB\u2081 + 1/2\u03BB\u2082].`},{pn:69,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"A photoelectric surface is illuminated successively by monochromatic light of wavelengths \u03BB and \u03BB/2. If the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons in the second case is 3 times that in the first case, the work function of the surface is",o:["hc/2\u03BB","hc/\u03BB","hc/3\u03BB","3hc/\u03BB"],c:0,s:`hc\xB72/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = 3(hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6)
2hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6 = 3hc/\u03BB \u2212 3\u03C6
2\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB, so \u03C6 = hc/2\u03BB.`},{pn:70,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:`In a historical experiment to determine Planck's constant, a metal surface was irradiated with light of different wavelengths. The emitted photoelectron energies were measured by applying a stopping potential. The relevant data for the wavelength (\u03BB) of incident light and the corresponding stopping potential (V\u2080) are given below:
\u03BB (\u03BCm): 0.3, 0.4, 0.5
V\u2080 (Volt): 2.0, 1.0, 0.4
Given that c = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m s\u207B\xB9 and e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, Planck's constant (in units of J s) found from such an experiment is`,o:["6.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074","6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074","6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074","6.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074"],c:1,s:`eV\u2080 = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6, so e(V\u2080\u2081 \u2212 V\u2080\u2082) = hc(1/\u03BB\u2081 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2082).
Take \u03BB = 0.3 and 0.4 \u03BCm: 1/0.3 \u2212 1/0.4 = 0.8333 \u03BCm\u207B\xB9 = 8.333 \xD7 10\u2075 m\u207B\xB9.
h = e \xD7 1.0 / (c \xD7 8.333 \xD7 10\u2075) = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 / 2.5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 = 6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s.
Check with 0.4 and 0.5 \u03BCm: 0.6 e / (c \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u2075) = 6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074.`},{pn:71,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A beam of light has two wavelengths 4972 \xC5 and 6216 \xC5 with a total intensity of 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 W m\u207B\xB2 equally distributed among the two wavelengths. The beam falls normally on an area of 1 cm\xB2 of a clean metallic surface of work function 2.3 eV. Assume that there is no loss of light by reflection and that each capable photon ejects one electron. The number of photoelectrons liberated in 2 s is approximately",o:["6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","9 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","11 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","15 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9"],c:1,s:`Photon energies: 1240/497.2 = 2.49 eV (above 2.3, ejects) and 1240/621.6 = 2.00 eV (below 2.3, cannot).
Power on the area from each wavelength = 1.8\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 1.8\xD710\u207B\u2077 W.
Energy of the 497.2 nm light in 2 s = 3.6\xD710\u207B\u2077 J.
N = 3.6\xD710\u207B\u2077 / (2.49 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) \u2248 9\xD710\xB9\xB9.`},{pn:72,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"A metal surface is illuminated by light of two different wavelengths 248 nm and 310 nm. The maximum speeds of the photoelectrons corresponding to these wavelengths are u\u2081 and u\u2082, respectively. If the ratio u\u2081 : u\u2082 = 2 : 1 and hc = 1240 eV nm, the work function of the metal is nearly",o:["3.7 eV","3.2 eV","2.8 eV","2.5 eV"],c:0,s:`Photon energies: 1240/248 = 5 eV and 1240/310 = 4 eV.
Speed ratio 2:1 means K\u2081 = 4K\u2082.
5 \u2212 \u03C6 = 4(4 \u2212 \u03C6), so 3\u03C6 = 11 and \u03C6 \u2248 3.7 eV.`},{pn:73,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"The anode voltage of a photocell is kept fixed. The wavelength \u03BB of the light falling on the cathode is gradually changed. The plate current I of the photocell varies as follows",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`As \u03BB increases the photon energy falls; beyond the threshold wavelength no electrons are emitted at all.
So the current is largest for short wavelengths and falls towards zero as \u03BB grows: the decreasing curve is (a).`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"Four graphs of plate current I against wavelength",d:[["l",22,86,162,86,"a"],["l",22,86,22,6,"a"],["t",10,14,"(a)","s"],["p","M27,14 Q42,66 147,82"],["l",212,86,352,86,"a"],["l",212,86,212,6,"a"],["t",200,14,"(b)","s"],["p","M217,82 C252,82 252,12 277,12 C302,12 302,82 337,82"],["l",22,184,162,184,"a"],["l",22,184,22,104,"a"],["t",10,112,"(c)","s"],["p","M27,180 L57,180 Q72,180 82,144 L147,140"],["l",212,184,352,184,"a"],["l",212,184,212,104,"a"],["t",200,112,"(d)","s"],["p","M217,180 Q247,144 262,128 L337,126"],["t",150,98,"\u03BB","s"],["t",340,98,"\u03BB","s"]]}},{pn:74,y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"eq",st:"Electron at the anode and charging of a plate",q:"A copper ball of radius 1 cm and work function 4.47 eV is irradiated with ultraviolet radiation of wavelength 2500 \xC5. The effect of irradiation results in the emission of electrons from the ball. Further the ball will acquire charge and due to this there will be a finite value of the potential on the ball. The charge acquired by the ball is",o:["5.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 C","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 C","4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 C"],c:0,s:`Photon energy = 1240/250 = 4.96 eV, so K_max = 4.96 \u2212 4.47 = 0.49 eV.
Emission stops when the ball reaches V = 0.49 V.
Q = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080RV = (0.01/9\xD710\u2079)(0.49) \u2248 5.5\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB3 C.`},{pn:75,y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"If a source of power 4 kW produces 10\xB2\u2070 photons/second, the radiation belongs to a part of the spectrum called",o:["\u03B3-rays","X-rays","ultraviolet rays","microwaves"],c:1,s:`Energy per photon = 4000/10\xB2\u2070 = 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2077 J = 250 eV.
\u03BB = 1240/250 \u2248 5 nm, which is in the X-ray range.`},{pn:76,y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The surface of a metal is illuminated with the light of 400 nm. The kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectrons was found to be 1.68 eV. The work function of the metal is (hc = 1240 eV nm)",o:["3.09 eV","1.41 eV","1.51 eV","1.68 eV"],c:1,s:`Photon energy = 1240/400 = 3.10 eV.
\u03C6 = E \u2212 K = 3.10 \u2212 1.68 = 1.42 eV, closest to 1.41 eV.`},{pn:77,y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"Photoelectric effect experiments are performed using three different metal plates p, q and r having work functions \u03C6_p = 2.0 eV, \u03C6_q = 2.5 eV and \u03C6_r = 3.0 eV respectively. A light beam containing wavelengths of 550 nm, 450 nm and 350 nm with equal intensities illuminates each of the plates. The correct I\u2013V graph for the experiment is (take hc = 1240 eV nm)",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`Photon energies: 550 nm = 2.25 eV, 450 nm = 2.76 eV, 350 nm = 3.54 eV.
Plate p (2.0 eV): all three wavelengths eject; K_max = 3.54 \u2212 2.0 = 1.54 eV.
Plate q (2.5 eV): 450 and 350 nm eject; K_max = 1.04 eV.
Plate r (3.0 eV): only 350 nm ejects; K_max = 0.54 eV.
So the stopping potential is largest for p, then q, then r, and the saturation current is also largest for p (most wavelengths contribute), then q, then r. Graph (a) shows this.`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"Four sets of I\u2013V curves for plates p, q, r",d:[["l",22,86,162,86,"a"],["l",82,86,82,6,"a"],["t",10,14,"(a)","s"],["p","M27,82 C47,82 42,18 67,18 L147,18"],["p","M42,82 C62,82 57,36 82,36 L147,36"],["p","M57,82 C77,82 72,56 97,56 L147,56"],["t",150,16,"p","s"],["t",150,34,"q","s"],["t",150,54,"r","s"],["l",212,86,352,86,"a"],["l",272,86,272,6,"a"],["t",200,14,"(b)","s"],["p","M242,82 C262,82 257,18 282,18 L337,18"],["p","M242,82 C262,82 257,36 282,36 L337,36"],["p","M242,82 C262,82 257,56 282,56 L337,56"],["t",340,16,"p","s"],["t",340,34,"q","s"],["t",340,54,"r","s"],["l",22,184,162,184,"a"],["l",82,184,82,104,"a"],["t",10,112,"(c)","s"],["p","M27,180 C47,180 42,124 67,124 L147,124"],["p","M42,180 C62,180 57,124 82,124 L147,124"],["p","M57,180 C77,180 72,124 97,124 L147,124"],["t",150,122,"p","s"],["t",150,122,"q","s"],["t",150,122,"r","s"],["l",212,184,352,184,"a"],["l",272,184,272,104,"a"],["t",200,112,"(d)","s"],["p","M247,180 C267,180 262,116 287,116 L337,116"],["p","M232,180 C252,180 247,134 272,134 L337,134"],["p","M217,180 C237,180 232,154 257,154 L337,154"],["t",340,114,"p","s"],["t",340,132,"q","s"],["t",340,152,"r","s"]]}},{pn:78,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A photon of frequency \u03C5 has a momentum associated with it. If c is the velocity of light, the momentum is",o:["h\u03C5/c","\u03C5/c","h\u03C5c","h\u03C5/c\xB2"],c:0,s:"Energy E = h\u03C5 and E = pc for a photon, so p = h\u03C5/c."},{pn:79,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"The anode voltage of a photocell is kept fixed. The wavelength \u03BB of the light falling on the cathode is gradually changed. The plate current I of the photocell varies as follows",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`As \u03BB increases the photon energy falls; beyond the threshold wavelength no electrons are emitted at all.
So the current falls as \u03BB grows and vanishes at long wavelength: the decreasing curve is (c).`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"Four graphs of plate current I against wavelength",d:[["l",22,86,162,86,"a"],["l",22,86,22,6,"a"],["t",10,14,"(a)","s"],["p","M27,82 L82,82 Q122,76 147,21"],["l",212,86,352,86,"a"],["l",212,86,212,6,"a"],["t",200,14,"(b)","s"],["p","M217,82 Q292,80 337,18"],["l",22,184,162,184,"a"],["l",22,184,22,104,"a"],["t",10,112,"(c)","s"],["p","M27,112 Q42,164 147,180"],["l",212,184,352,184,"a"],["l",212,184,212,104,"a"],["t",200,112,"(d)","s"],["p","M217,180 C252,180 252,110 277,110 C302,110 302,180 337,180"]]}},{pn:80,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The threshold frequency for a metallic surface corresponds to an energy of 6.2 eV, and the stopping potential for a radiation incident on this surface is 5 V. The incident radiation lies in",o:["X-ray region","ultra-violet region","infra-red region","visible region"],c:1,s:`Photon energy = \u03C6 + eV\u2080 = 6.2 + 5 = 11.2 eV.
\u03BB = 1240/11.2 \u2248 111 nm, which is ultraviolet (X-rays are below about 10 nm).`},{pn:81,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"The time taken by a photoelectron to come out after the photon strikes is approximately",o:["10\u207B\xB9 s","10\u207B\u2074 s","10\u207B\xB9\u2070 s","10\u207B\xB9\u2076 s"],c:2,s:"Photoemission is practically instantaneous (no measurable delay), of the order of 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 s or less; among the options 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 s is the accepted answer."},{pn:82,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"A photocell is illuminated by a small bright source placed 1 m away. When the same source of light is placed (1/2) m away, the number of electrons emitted by photocathode would",o:["decrease by a factor of 2","increase by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 4","increase by a factor of 4"],c:3,s:`Intensity from a small source varies as 1/r\xB2.
Distance halves, so intensity becomes 4 times.
Number of photons hitting the cathode per second is proportional to intensity, so emitted electrons increase by a factor of 4.`},{pn:83,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A photon collides with a stationary hydrogen atom in ground state inelastically. Energy of the colliding photon is 10.2 eV. After a time interval of the order of micro second another photon collides with the same hydrogen atom inelastically with an energy of 15 eV. What will be observed by the detector?",o:["One photon of energy 10.2 eV and an electron of energy 1.4 eV","Two photons of energy 1.4 eV","Two photons of energy 10.2 eV","One photon of energy 10.2 eV and another photon of 1.4 eV"],c:0,s:`10.2 eV lifts the atom from n = 1 to n = 2. Its lifetime (about 10\u207B\u2078 s) is much shorter than a microsecond, so it has already returned to the ground state emitting a 10.2 eV photon.
The second photon (15 eV) meets a ground-state atom and ionises it (13.6 eV needed).
Electron energy = 15 \u2212 13.6 = 1.4 eV.
Detector sees a 10.2 eV photon and a 1.4 eV electron.`},{pn:84,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The work function of a substance is 4.0 eV. The longest wavelength of light that can cause photoelectron emission from this substance is approximately",o:["540 nm","400 nm","310 nm","220 nm"],c:2,s:"\u03BB_max = hc/\u03C6 = 1240/4.0 = 310 nm."},{pn:85,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"According to Einstein's photoelectric equation, the plot of the kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons from a metal vs the frequency of the incident radiation gives a straight line whose slope",o:["depends on the nature of the metal used","depends on the intensity of the radiation","depends both on the intensity of the radiation and the metal used","is the same for all metals and independent of the intensity of the radiation"],c:3,s:`K_max = hf \u2212 \u03C6 is a straight line of slope h.
h is a universal constant, so the slope is the same for every metal and every intensity.`},{pn:86,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"In a photoelectric experiment anode potential is plotted against plate current. A, B and C are three curves, as shown.",o:["A and B will have same intensities while B and C will have different frequencies","B and C will have different intensities while A and B will have different frequencies","A and B will have different intensities while B and C will have equal frequencies","B and C will have equal intensities while A and B will have same frequencies"],c:3,s:`Stopping potential reveals frequency: A and B cut off at the same potential, so they have the same frequency; C has a larger stopping potential, so a higher frequency.
Saturation current reveals intensity: B and C reach the same saturation current, so equal intensities; A saturates lower.
This is statement (d).`,fig:{w:270,h:170,alt:"Three I\u2013V curves A, B, C with the anode potential on the horizontal axis",d:[["l",10,140,250,140,"a"],["l",90,150,90,10,"a"],["t",245,155,"V","s"],["t",96,16,"I","s"],["p","M20,140 C40,140 36,35 66,35 L220,35"],["p","M50,140 C70,140 66,35 96,35 L220,35"],["p","M50,140 C65,140 62,82 84,82 L220,82"],["t",224,39,"B, C","s"],["t",224,86,"A","s"],["t",8,120,"C","s"],["t",54,128,"A, B","s"]]}},{pn:87,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"Two identical photocathodes receive light of frequencies f\u2081 and f\u2082. If the velocities of the photoelectrons (of mass m) coming out are respectively v\u2081 and v\u2082, then",o:["v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2 = (2h/m)(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082)","v\u2081 + v\u2082 = [(2h/m)(f\u2081 + f\u2082)]^(1/2)","v\u2081\xB2 + v\u2082\xB2 = (2h/m)(f\u2081 + f\u2082)","v\u2081 \u2212 v\u2082 = [(2h/m)(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082)]^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`\xBDmv\u2081\xB2 = hf\u2081 \u2212 \u03C6 and \xBDmv\u2082\xB2 = hf\u2082 \u2212 \u03C6.
Subtract: \xBDm(v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2) = h(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082), so v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2 = (2h/m)(f\u2081 \u2212 f\u2082).`},{pn:88,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"Sodium and copper have work functions 2.3 eV and 4.5 eV respectively. Then the ratio of the (threshold) wavelengths is nearest to",o:["1 : 2","4 : 1","2 : 1","1 : 4"],c:2,s:`\u03BB\u2080 = hc/\u03C6 \u221D 1/\u03C6.
\u03BB_Na : \u03BB_Cu = 4.5 : 2.3 \u2248 2 : 1.`},{pn:89,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons emitted from a surface when photons of energy 6 eV fall on it is 4 eV. The stopping potential, in volt, is",o:["2","4","6","10"],c:1,s:"eV\u2080 = K_max = 4 eV, so V\u2080 = 4 V."},{pn:90,y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"The filament of a light bulb has surface area 64 mm\xB2. The filament can be considered as a black body at temperature 2500 K emitting radiation like a point source when viewed from far. At night the light bulb is observed from a distance of 100 m. Assume the pupil of the eyes of the observer to be circular with radius 3 mm. Then (Stefan\u2013Boltzmann constant = 5.67 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W m\u207B\xB2 K\u207B\u2074, Wien's displacement constant = 2.90 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m K, Planck's constant = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, speed of light in vacuum = 3.00 \xD7 10\u2078 m s\u207B\xB9)",o:["power radiated by the filament is in the range 642 W to 645 W","radiated power entering into one eye of the observer is in the range 3.15 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W to 3.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W","the wavelength corresponding to the maximum intensity of light is 1160 nm","taking the average wavelength of emitted radiation to be 1740 nm, the total number of photons entering per second into one eye of the observer is in the range 2.75 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 to 2.85 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9"],c:[1,2,3],s:`(a) P = \u03C3AT\u2074 = 5.67\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 64\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (2500)\u2074 \u2248 142 W, so (a) is false.
(b) Intensity at 100 m = 142/(4\u03C0\xD710\u2074) = 1.13\xD710\u207B\xB3 W/m\xB2; pupil area = \u03C0(3\xD710\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 2.83\xD710\u207B\u2075 m\xB2; power = 3.2\xD710\u207B\u2078 W. True.
(c) \u03BB_m = 2.90\xD710\u207B\xB3/2500 = 1160 nm. True.
(d) Photon energy = hc/1740 nm = 1.14\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J; N = 3.2\xD710\u207B\u2078/1.14\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2248 2.8\xD710\xB9\xB9 per second. True.`},{pn:91,y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:`For the photoelectric effect with incident photon wavelength \u03BB, the stopping potential is V\u2080. Identify the correct variation(s) of V\u2080 with \u03BB and 1/\u03BB.
(a) V\u2080 against \u03BB: a curve falling steeply and flattening
(b) V\u2080 against \u03BB: a straight line falling
(c) V\u2080 against 1/\u03BB: a straight line rising from a point on the 1/\u03BB axis
(d) V\u2080 against 1/\u03BB: a curve rising from the 1/\u03BB axis (concave up)`,o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:[0,2],s:`eV\u2080 = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6.
Against \u03BB this is a hyperbola-type falling curve, not a straight line: (a) fits, (b) does not.
Against 1/\u03BB it is a straight line of slope hc/e with a positive intercept on the 1/\u03BB axis (1/\u03BB\u2080): (c) fits, (d) does not.`,fig:{w:300,h:212,alt:"Four graphs: (a) V0 against lambda falling curve, (b) V0 against lambda straight falling line, (c) V0 against 1/lambda straight rising line, (d) V0 against 1/lambda rising curve",d:[["l",30,90,130,90,"a"],["l",30,90,30,10,"a"],["p","M36 20 Q46 82 125 88"],["t",16,16,"V\u2080","s"],["t",120,104,"\u03BB","s"],["t",70,104,"(a)","s"],["l",190,90,290,90,"a"],["l",190,90,190,10,"a"],["p","M196 20 L250 88"],["t",176,16,"V\u2080","s"],["t",280,104,"\u03BB","s"],["t",230,104,"(b)","s"],["l",30,190,130,190,"a"],["l",30,190,30,110,"a"],["p","M55 190 L95 120"],["t",16,116,"V\u2080","s"],["t",112,204,"1/\u03BB","s"],["t",70,204,"(c)","s"],["l",190,190,290,190,"a"],["l",190,190,190,110,"a"],["p","M205 190 Q250 190 270 122"],["t",176,116,"V\u2080","s"],["t",272,204,"1/\u03BB","s"],["t",230,204,"(d)","s"]]}},{pn:92,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:`The graph between the stopping potential (V\u2080) and (1/\u03BB) is shown in the figure. \u03C6\u2081, \u03C6\u2082 and \u03C6\u2083 are work functions. Which of the following is/are correct?
(The lines for Metal 1, Metal 2 and Metal 3 are parallel; they cut the 1/\u03BB axis at 0.001, 0.002 and 0.004 nm\u207B\xB9 respectively; \u03B8 is the angle of the line of Metal 1 with the 1/\u03BB axis.)`,o:["\u03C6\u2081 : \u03C6\u2082 : \u03C6\u2083 = 1 : 2 : 4","\u03C6\u2081 : \u03C6\u2082 : \u03C6\u2083 = 4 : 2 : 1","tan \u03B8 is directly proportional to hc/e, where h is Planck's constant and c is the speed of light","Ultra violet light can be used to emit photoelectrons from metal 2 and metal 3 only."],c:[0,2],s:`eV\u2080 = hc(1/\u03BB) \u2212 \u03C6. The 1/\u03BB-intercept is 1/\u03BB\u2080 = \u03C6/hc, so \u03C6 is proportional to the intercept: 0.001 : 0.002 : 0.004 = 1 : 2 : 4. (a) true, (b) false.
Slope of V\u2080 against 1/\u03BB is hc/e, the same for all metals, so tan \u03B8 is proportional to hc/e. (c) true.
Threshold wavelengths: 1000 nm, 500 nm, 250 nm. UV (below 400 nm) exceeds the threshold only for Metal 3, not Metal 2. (d) false.`,fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Graph of stopping potential V0 against 1/lambda: three parallel straight lines for Metals 1, 2 and 3 cutting the 1/lambda axis at 0.001, 0.002 and 0.004 per nm, with angle theta marked on Metal 1",d:[["l",30,140,30,12,"a"],["l",30,140,250,140,"a"],["t",22,16,"V\u2080","e"],["t",252,144,"1/\u03BB (nm\u207B\xB9)",""],["l",70,140,105,40,""],["l",110,140,145,40,""],["l",190,140,225,40,""],["t",90,32,"Metal 1","m"],["t",130,32,"Metal 2","m"],["t",212,32,"Metal 3","m"],["t",70,155,"0.001","m"],["t",110,155,"0.002","m"],["t",190,155,"0.004","m"],["p","M84 140A14 14 0 0 0 82 131",""],["t",90,136,"\u03B8","s"],["t",72,112,"\u03C6\u2081","s"],["t",146,112,"\u03C6\u2082","s"],["t",226,112,"\u03C6\u2083","s"]]}},{pn:93,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The work function of a substance is 4.0 eV. The longest wavelength of light that can cause photoelectron emission from this substance is approximately",o:["540 nm","400 nm","310 nm","220 nm"],c:2,s:"\u03BB\u2080 = hc/\u03C6 = 1240/4.0 nm = 310 nm."},{pn:94,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:"When a monochromatic point source of light is at a distance of 0.2 m from a photoelectric cell, the cut-off voltage and the saturation current are 0.6 V and 18.0 mA respectively. If the same source is placed 0.6 m away from the photoelectric cell, then",o:["the stopping potential will be 0.2 volt","the stopping potential will be 0.6 volt","the saturation current will be 6.0 mA","the saturation current will be 2.0 mA"],c:[1,3],s:`Stopping potential depends on frequency only, so it stays 0.6 V.
Intensity \u221D 1/r\xB2; distance is 3 times, so intensity is 1/9 and the saturation current is 18/9 = 2.0 mA.`},{pn:95,y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"Photoelectric effect supports the quantum nature of light because",o:["there is a minimum frequency of light below which no photoelectrons are emitted","the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons depends only on the frequency of light and not on its intensity","even when the metal surface is faintly illuminated, the photoelectrons leave the surface immediately","the electric charge of the photoelectrons is quantised"],c:[0,1,2],s:`(a) threshold frequency, (b) Kmax independent of intensity, (c) instantaneous emission even for faint light: none of these fit the wave picture, so all support quanta.
(d) the electron charge being quantised has nothing to do with light.`},{pn:96,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"The threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a material is 5200 \xC5. Photoelectrons will be emitted when this material is illuminated with monochromatic radiation from a",o:["50 watt infrared lamp","1 watt infrared lamp","50 watt ultraviolet lamp","1 watt ultraviolet lamp"],c:[2,3],s:`Infrared has \u03BB > 5200 \xC5, so no photon has enough energy, whatever the power: (a), (b) fail.
Ultraviolet has \u03BB < 5200 \xC5, so each photon can eject an electron; power only changes the number emitted: (c) and (d) both work.
(The printed key is blank; the answer is established by this working.)`},{pn:97,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"The ratio of the power of a light source S\u2081 to that the light source S\u2082 is 2. S\u2081 is emitting 2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 photons per second at 600 nm. If the wavelength of the source S\u2082 is 300 nm, then the number of photons per second emitted by S\u2082 is ___ \xD7 10\xB9\u2074.",n:5,s:`Power = (photons per second) \xD7 hc/\u03BB.
P\u2081 = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 \xD7 hc/600, and P\u2082 = P\u2081/2 = 10\xB9\u2075 \xD7 hc/600.
N\u2082 = P\u2082 / (hc/300) = 10\xB9\u2075 \xD7 300/600 = 5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074.
So the blank is 5.`},{pn:98,y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A cube of unit volume contains 35 \xD7 10\u2077 photons of frequency 10\xB9\u2075 Hz. If the energy of all the photons is viewed as the average energy contained in the electromagnetic waves within the same volume, then taking the amplitude of the magnetic field as \u03B1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 T. Taking the permeability of free space \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m/A, Planck's constant h = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s and \u03C0 = 22/7, the value of \u03B1 is _____.",n:22.98,s:`Energy density u = 35\xD710\u2077 \xD7 6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 = 2.1\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 J/m\xB3.
Average u = B\u2080\xB2/(2\u03BC\u2080) \u21D2 B\u2080\xB2 = 2\u03BC\u2080u = 2 \xD7 4\xD7(22/7)\xD710\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2.1\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 = 5.28\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2076.
B\u2080 \u2248 2.298\xD710\u207B\u2078 T = 22.98\xD710\u207B\u2079 T, so \u03B1 \u2248 22.98.`},{pn:99,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"In the Franck\u2013Hertz experiment, the first dip in the current\u2013voltage graph for hydrogen is observed at 10.2 V. The wavelength of light emitted by a hydrogen atom when excited to the first excitation level is _____ nm. (Given hc = 1245 eV nm, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",n:122,s:`Electrons gain 10.2 eV, which excites the atom to n = 2; on de-excitation it emits a 10.2 eV photon.
\u03BB = 1245/10.2 \u2248 122 nm.`},{pn:100,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"When light of frequency twice the threshold frequency is incident on the metal plate, the maximum velocity of emitted electrons is v\u2081. When the frequency of incident radiation is increased to five times the threshold value, the maximum velocity of emitted electrons becomes v\u2082. If v\u2082 = xv\u2081, the value of x will be _____.",n:2,s:`Kmax = h\u03BD \u2212 h\u03BD\u2080.
At 2\u03BD\u2080: Kmax = h\u03BD\u2080. At 5\u03BD\u2080: Kmax = 4h\u03BD\u2080.
v \u221D \u221AK, so v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A4 = 2.`},{pn:101,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"The stopping potential for photoelectrons emitted from a surface illuminated by light of wavelength 6630 \xC5 is 0.42 V. If the threshold frequency is x \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 /s, where x is _____ (nearest integer). (Given: speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s, Planck's constant = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",n:35,s:`\u03BD = c/\u03BB = 3\xD710\u2078/6.63\xD710\u207B\u2077 = 4.52\xD710\xB9\u2074 Hz; h\u03BD = 3.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
eV\u2080 = 0.42 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 0.672\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
h\u03BD\u2080 = 3.0\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2212 0.672\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 2.328\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J \u21D2 \u03BD\u2080 = 3.51\xD710\xB9\u2074 = 35.1\xD710\xB9\xB3, so x = 35.`},{pn:102,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"In a photoemission experiment, the maximum kinetic energies of photoelectrons from metals P, Q and R are E_P, E_Q and E_R respectively, and they are related by E_P = 2E_Q = 2E_R. In this experiment, the same source of monochromatic light is used for metals P and Q while a different source of monochromatic light is used for the metal R. The work functions for metals P, Q and R are 4.0 eV, 4.5 eV and 5.5 eV respectively. The energy of the incident photon used for metal R, in eV, is _____.",n:6,s:`Same photon E for P and Q: E \u2212 4.0 = 2(E \u2212 4.5) \u21D2 E = 5.0 eV.
So E_P = 1.0 eV, E_Q = 0.5 eV = E_R.
For R: photon energy = E_R + \u03C6_R = 0.5 + 5.5 = 6 eV.`},{pn:103,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"A certain metallic surface is illuminated by monochromatic radiation of wavelength \u03BB. The stopping potential for photoelectric current for this radiation is 3V\u2080. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength 2\u03BB, the stopping potential is V\u2080. The threshold wavelength of this surface for photoelectric effect is _____ \u03BB.",n:4,s:`Let E = hc/\u03BB (in eV units): E = \u03C6 + 3V\u2080 and E/2 = \u03C6 + V\u2080.
Subtract: E/2 = 2V\u2080 \u21D2 E = 4V\u2080, \u03C6 = V\u2080.
\u03BB\u2080 = hc/\u03C6 = hc/V\u2080 = 4\u03BB. Answer 4.`},{pn:104,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A light beam of wavelength 500 nm is incident on a metal having work function of 1.25 eV, placed in a magnetic field of intensity B. The electrons emitted perpendicular to the magnetic field B, with maximum kinetic energy, are bent into a circular arc of radius 30 cm. The value of B is _____ \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T. (Given hc = 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 J m, mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",n:125,s:`Photon energy = 20\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2076/500\xD710\u207B\u2079 = 4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J; \u03C6 = 1.25\xD71.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
Kmax = 2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J, p = \u221A(2mK) = \u221A(2\xD79\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9\xD72\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 6\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2075 kg m/s.
r = p/(eB) \u21D2 B = 6\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2075/(1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.3) = 1.25\xD710\u207B\u2075 T = 125 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.`},{pn:105,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"Two streams of photons, possessing energies equal to twice and ten times the work function of a metal, are incident on the metal surface successively. The value of the ratio of the maximum velocities of the photoelectrons emitted in the two respective cases is x : y. The value of x is ___.",n:1,s:`K = E \u2212 \u03C6.
Case 1: K\u2081 = 2\u03C6 \u2212 \u03C6 = \u03C6. Case 2: K\u2082 = 10\u03C6 \u2212 \u03C6 = 9\u03C6.
v \u221D \u221AK, so v\u2081 : v\u2082 = \u221A\u03C6 : \u221A(9\u03C6) = 1 : 3.
Hence x = 1.`},{pn:106,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When radiation of wavelength \u03BB is used to illuminate a metallic surface, the stopping potential is V. When the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength 3\u03BB, the stopping potential is V/4. If the threshold wavelength for the metallic surface is n\u03BB, then the value of n will be _____.",n:9,s:`With E = hc/\u03BB: E = \u03C6 + V and E/3 = \u03C6 + V/4.
Subtract: 2E/3 = 3V/4 \u21D2 E = 9V/8, so \u03C6 = E \u2212 V = V/8 = E/9.
\u03C6 = hc/\u03BB\u2080 = E/9 \u21D2 \u03BB\u2080 = 9\u03BB, n = 9.`},{pn:107,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"The surface of a metal is illuminated alternately with photons of energies E\u2081 = 4 eV and E\u2082 = 2.5 eV respectively. The ratio of the maximum speeds of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases is 2. The work function of the metal (in eV) is _____.",n:2,s:`Speed ratio 2 \u21D2 Kmax ratio 4: (4 \u2212 \u03C6) = 4(2.5 \u2212 \u03C6).
4 \u2212 \u03C6 = 10 \u2212 4\u03C6 \u21D2 3\u03C6 = 6 \u21D2 \u03C6 = 2 eV.`},{pn:108,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"A beam of electromagnetic radiation of intensity 6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 W/cm\xB2 is comprised of wavelength \u03BB = 310 nm. It falls normally on a metal (work function \u03C6 = 2 eV) of surface area 1 cm\xB2. If one in 10\xB3 photons ejects an electron, the total number of electrons ejected in 1 s is 10\u02E3. Then x is _____. (hc = 1240 eV nm, 1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J)",n:11,s:`Photon energy = 1240/310 = 4 eV = 6.4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
Power on 1 cm\xB2 = 6.4\xD710\u207B\u2075 W, so photons per second = 6.4\xD710\u207B\u2075/6.4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 10\xB9\u2074.
Electrons = 10\xB9\u2074/10\xB3 = 10\xB9\xB9, so x = 11.`},{pn:109,y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"photon",st:"Force and pressure of light",q:"A perfectly reflecting mirror of mass M, mounted on a spring, constitutes a spring\u2013mass system of angular frequency \u03A9 such that 4\u03C0M\u03A9/h = 10\xB2\u2074 m\u207B\xB2 with h as Planck's constant. N photons of wavelength \u03BB = 8\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m strike the mirror simultaneously at normal incidence such that the mirror gets displaced by 1 \u03BCm. If the value of N is x \xD7 10\xB9\xB2, then the value of x is _____. [Consider the spring as massless]",n:1,s:`Each photon reflects, giving momentum 2h/\u03BB; all N at once: Mv = 2Nh/\u03BB.
The mirror then does SHM from equilibrium with amplitude A = v/\u03A9 = 1 \u03BCm.
N = \u03BBM\u03A9A/(2h) = (8\u03C0\xD710\u207B\u2076)(M\u03A9/h)(10\u207B\u2076)/2, with M\u03A9/h = 10\xB2\u2074/(4\u03C0).
N = 8\u03C0\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\xB2\u2074/(4\u03C0) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 / 2 = 10\xB9\xB2, so x = 1.`,fig:{w:300,h:130,alt:"Light travelling right strikes a mirror attached to a spring fixed to a wall",d:[["l",20,65,172,65,"a"],["t",60,50,"Light","s"],["l",180,25,180,105,"B"],["t",160,122,"Mirror","s"],["p","M180 65 L195 65 L200 50 L210 80 L220 50 L230 80 L240 50 L250 80 L255 65 L280 65"],["l",280,30,280,100,"B"],["t",215,40,"spring","s"]]}},{pn:110,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"photon",st:"Force and pressure of light",q:"In a photoelectric experiment a parallel beam of monochromatic light with power of 200 W is incident on a perfectly absorbing cathode of work function 6.25 eV. The frequency of light is just above the threshold frequency so that the photoelectrons are emitted with negligible kinetic energy. Assume that the photoelectron emission efficiency is 100%. A potential difference of 500 V is applied between the cathode and the anode. All the emitted electrons are incident normally on the anode and are absorbed. The anode experiences a force F = n \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N due to the impact of the electrons. The value of n is ___. Mass of the electron m\u2091 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg and 1.0 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.",n:24,s:`Photon energy \u2248 \u03C6 = 6.25 eV = 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 J, so electrons per second = 200 / 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 = 2 \xD7 10\xB2\u2070.
Electron reaches the anode with K = 500 eV = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077 J.
p = \u221A(2mK) = \u221A(2 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077) = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 kg m/s.
Electrons are absorbed, so F = (rate) \xD7 p = 2 \xD7 10\xB2\u2070 \xD7 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N = 24 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N.
n = 24.`},{pn:111,y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"The work functions of Silver and Sodium are 4.6 eV and 2.3 eV, respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for Silver to that of Sodium is ___.",n:1,s:`eV\u2080 = hf \u2212 \u03C6, so V\u2080 = (h/e) f \u2212 \u03C6/e.
The slope h/e is a universal constant and does not depend on the work function.
So the ratio of slopes is 1.`},{pn:112,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"eq",st:"Electron at the anode and charging of a plate",q:"A silver sphere of radius 1 cm and work function 4.7 eV is suspended from an insulating thread in free-space. It is under continuous illumination of 200 nm wavelength light. As photoelectrons are emitted, the sphere gets charged and acquires a potential. The maximum number of photoelectrons emitted from the sphere is A \xD7 10\u1DBB (where 1 < A < 10). The value of Z is ___.",n:7,s:`Photon energy = 1240/200 = 6.2 eV, so Kmax at the start = 6.2 \u2212 4.7 = 1.5 eV.
Emission stops when the sphere potential reaches V = 1.5 V.
Q = 4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080RV = (0.01 \xD7 1.5)/(9 \xD7 10\u2079) = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C.
N = Q/e = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 / 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \u2248 1.04 \xD7 10\u2077.
So A \u2248 1.04 and Z = 7.`},{pn:113,y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:`Match the temperature of a black body given in List-I with an appropriate statement in List-II, and choose the correct option. [Given: Wien's constant = 2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m K and hc/e = 1.24 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 V m]
List-I: (P) 2000 K  (Q) 3000 K  (R) 5000 K  (S) 10000 K
List-II:
1. The radiation at peak wavelength can lead to emission of photoelectrons from a metal of work function 4 eV.
2. The radiation at peak wavelength is visible to the human eye.
3. The radiation at peak emission wavelength will result in the widest central maximum of a single slit diffraction.
4. The power emitted per unit area is 1/16 of that emitted by a blackbody at temperature 6000 K.
5. The radiation at peak emission wavelength can be used to image human bones.`,o:["P\u21923; Q\u21925; R\u21922; S\u21923","P\u21923; Q\u21922; R\u21924; S\u21921","P\u21923; Q\u21924; R\u21922; S\u21921","P\u21921; Q\u21922; R\u21925; S\u21923"],c:2,s:`\u03BB_peak = 2.9\xD710\u207B\xB3/T: P 1450 nm, Q 967 nm, R 580 nm, S 290 nm.
S: photon energy = 1240/290 \u2248 4.3 eV > 4 eV, so 1. R: 580 nm is visible, so 2.
Widest central maximum needs the longest \u03BB: P (2000 K), so 3.
Power \u221D T\u2074: (T/6000)\u2074 = 1/16 \u21D2 T = 3000 K, so Q \u2192 4. Item 5 (X-rays) matches none.
P\u21923, Q\u21924, R\u21922, S\u21921.`},{pn:114,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Assertion (A): In the photoelectric effect, on increasing the intensity of incident light the stopping potential increases.
Reason (R): Increase in intensity of light increases the rate of photoelectrons emitted, provided the frequency of incident light is greater than the threshold frequency.`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A"],c:1,s:`Stopping potential depends on frequency, not intensity, so A is false.
R is true: more photons per second, more electrons per second.`},{pn:115,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): Emission of electrons in photoelectric effect can be suppressed by applying a sufficiently negative electron potential to the photoemissive substance.
Reason (R): A negative electric potential, which stops the emission of electrons from the surface of a photoemissive substance, varies linearly with frequency of incident radiation.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],c:3,s:`(A) is true: a large enough retarding potential stops even the fastest electrons from reaching the collector.
(R) is true: V\u2080 = (h/e)f \u2212 \u03C6/e is linear in f.
But linearity with frequency does not explain why a negative potential suppresses emission; the reason is that the potential opposes the electrons' kinetic energy.
So both true, (R) not the explanation.`},{pn:116,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Assertion A: Number of photons increases with increase in frequency of light.
Reason R: Maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons increases with the frequency of incident radiation.`,o:["A is correct but R is not correct","Both A and R are correct and R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","A is not correct but R is correct"],c:3,s:`Photon count depends on intensity (and falls, for fixed power, as frequency rises), so A is not correct.
Kmax = h\u03BD \u2212 \u03C6 rises with \u03BD, so R is correct.`},{pn:117,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Figure shows the variation of stopping potential (V\u2080) with frequency (\u03C5) for the two photosensitive materials M\u2081 and M\u2082. The slope gives value of h/e, where h is Planck's constant, e is the charge of electron.
Statement II: M\u2082 will emit photoelectrons of greater kinetic energy for the incident radiation having same frequency.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct."],c:1,s:`V\u2080 = (h/e)\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6/e, so the slope is h/e: Statement I is correct.
The two lines are parallel, and M\u2081's line cuts the \u03C5 axis at a lower frequency, so M\u2081 has the smaller work function.
At the same frequency K = h\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6 is larger for M\u2081, not M\u2082. Statement II is incorrect.`,fig:{w:200,h:150,alt:"Graph of stopping potential V0 against frequency: two parallel straight lines, M1 on the left and M2 on the right, both rising to the right",d:[["l",30,125,30,12,"a"],["l",30,125,185,125,"a"],["t",22,16,"V\u2080","e"],["t",187,129,"\u03C5",""],["l",65,125,105,35,""],["l",100,125,140,35,""],["t",105,28,"M\u2081","m"],["t",142,28,"M\u2082","m"]]}},{pn:118,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Out of microwaves, infrared rays and ultraviolet rays, ultraviolet rays are the most effective for the emission of electrons from a metallic surface.
Statement II: Above the threshold frequency, the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is inversely proportional to the frequency of the incident light.
In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:1,s:`Ultraviolet has the highest frequency (largest photon energy) of the three, so it is the most effective: Statement I is true.
Kmax = hf \u2212 \u03C6 increases linearly with f; it is not inversely proportional. Statement II is false.`},{pn:119,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Statement I: Stopping potential in the photoelectric effect does not depend on the power of the light source.
Statement II: For a given metal, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectron depends on the wavelength of the incident light.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],c:2,s:"eV\u2080 = Kmax = hc/\u03BB \u2212 \u03C6: it depends on wavelength and not on power. Both statements are correct."},{pn:120,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`Assertion A: The photoelectric effect does not take place if the energy of the incident radiation is less than the work function of a metal.
Reason R: Kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is zero if the energy of the incident radiation is equal to the work function of a metal.`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct"],c:1,s:`Both are true (Kmax = E \u2212 \u03C6 is zero at E = \u03C6, and no emission for E < \u03C6).
But the zero kinetic energy at E = \u03C6 does not explain why E < \u03C6 gives no emission; the reason is that the photon cannot supply the escape energy, so R is not the explanation.`},{pn:121,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:`This question has Statement-1 and Statement-2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.
Statement-1: A metallic surface is irradiated by a monochromatic light of frequency \u03C5 > \u03C5\u2080 (the threshold frequency). The maximum kinetic energy and the stopping potential are Kmax and V\u2080 respectively. If the frequency incident on the surface is doubled, both Kmax and V\u2080 are also doubled.
Statement-2: The maximum kinetic energy and the stopping potential of photoelectrons emitted from a surface are linearly dependent on the frequency of incident light.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true."],c:3,s:`Kmax = h\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6 = eV\u2080.
The dependence on \u03C5 is linear but has a negative intercept \u2212\u03C6, so Kmax is not proportional to \u03C5.
Doubling \u03C5 gives 2h\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6, which is more than 2Kmax. Statement-1 is false.
Statement-2 (linear dependence) is true.`},{pn:122,y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:`This question has Statement-1 and Statement-2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.
Statement-1: When ultraviolet light is incident on a photocell, its stopping potential is V\u2080 and the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is Kmax. When the ultraviolet light is replaced by X-rays, both V\u2080 and Kmax increase.
Statement-2: Photoelectrons are emitted with speeds ranging from zero to a maximum value because of the range of frequencies present in the incident light.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not the correct explanation of Statement-1.","Statement-1 is false, Statement-2 is true."],c:0,s:`X-rays have higher frequency than UV, so Kmax = h\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6 and V\u2080 = Kmax/e both increase. Statement-1 is true.
The spread of speeds from zero to a maximum arises because electrons lose different amounts of energy inside the metal, not because of a range of frequencies; the light is monochromatic.
Statement-2 is false.`},{pn:123,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:`In a photoelectric experiment setup, photons of energy 5 eV fall on the cathode having work function 3 eV.
(a) If the saturation current is iA = 4 \u03BCA for intensity 10\u207B\u2075 W/m\xB2, then plot a graph between anode potential and current.
(b) Also draw a graph for intensity of incident radiation 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 W/m\xB2.
(Enter the saturation current of the graph in part (b), in \u03BCA.)`,s:`Kmax = 5 \u2212 3 = 2 eV, so the stopping potential is \u22122 V. The graph (a) is a curve with current 0 at V = \u22122 V, rising to a flat saturation value of 4 \u03BCA for positive anode potential.
Saturation current is proportional to intensity (number of photons per second).
Doubling the intensity doubles it: 8 \u03BCA. Graph (b) has the same cut-off at \u22122 V but saturates at 8 \u03BCA.`,a:"(a) Current is zero at V = \u22122 V and rises to a flat saturation current of 4 \u03BCA for positive anode potential. (b) The same cut-off at \u22122 V, with a saturation current of 8 \u03BCA."},{pn:124,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"eq",st:"Electron at the anode and charging of a plate",q:`Two metallic plates A and B, each of area 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, are placed parallel to each other at a separation of 1 cm. Plate B carries a positive charge of 33.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C. A monochromatic beam of light, with photons of energy 5 eV each, starts falling on plate A at t = 0 so that 10\xB9\u2076 photons fall on it per square metre per second. Assume that one photoelectron is emitted for every 10\u2076 incident photons. Also assume that all the emitted photoelectrons are collected by plate B and the work function of plate A remains constant at the value 2 eV. Determine
(a) the number of photoelectrons emitted up to t = 10 s,
(b) the magnitude of the electric field between the plates A and B at t = 10 s, and
(c) the kinetic energy of the most energetic photoelectron emitted at t = 10 s when it reaches plate B.
Neglect the time taken by the photoelectron to reach plate B. (Take \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2/N-m\xB2.)
(Enter the answer to part (c), in eV.)`,s:`(a) Photons per second on A = 10\xB9\u2076 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 = 5\xD710\xB9\xB2; electrons per second = 5\xD710\u2076; in 10 s: 5\xD710\u2077.
(b) Charge moved = 5\xD710\u2077 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 8\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 C. A becomes +8\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 C, B becomes 33.7\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \u2212 8\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 = 25.7\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 C.
E = (QB \u2212 QA)/(2\u03B5\u2080A) = 17.7\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2/(2 \xD7 8.85\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2074) = 2\xD710\xB3 N/C, from B towards A.
(c) Emission energy = 5 \u2212 2 = 3 eV. The field accelerates electrons towards B, adding eEd = 2000 \xD7 0.01 = 20 eV.
Maximum kinetic energy at B = 23 eV.`,a:"(a) 5 \xD7 10\u2077 photoelectrons (b) 2 \xD7 10\xB3 N/C (c) 23 eV"},{pn:125,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"When a beam of 10.6 eV photons of intensity 2.0 W/m\xB2 falls on a platinum surface of area 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 and work function 5.6 eV, 0.53% of the incident photons eject photoelectrons. Find the number of photoelectrons emitted per second and their minimum and maximum energies (in eV). [Take 1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J]",s:`Power on the surface = 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 W = 2\xD710\u207B\u2074 J/s.
Photon energy = 10.6 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 1.696\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2078 J, so photons per second = 1.18\xD710\xB9\u2074.
Electrons per second = 0.0053 \xD7 1.18\xD710\xB9\u2074 \u2248 6.25\xD710\xB9\xB9.
Maximum energy = 10.6 \u2212 5.6 = 5.0 eV; the minimum energy is 0 (electrons from deeper layers lose energy before leaving).`,a:"6.25 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 photoelectrons per second; minimum energy 0 eV, maximum energy 5.0 eV"},{pn:126,y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"photo",st:"Current, intensity and I\u2013V curves",q:`A monochromatic point source radiating wavelength 6000 \xC5, with power 2 watt, an aperture A of diameter 0.1 m and a large screen SC are placed as shown in the figure. A photoemissive detector D of surface area 0.5 cm\xB2 is placed at the centre of the screen. The efficiency of the detector for the photoelectron generation per incident photon is 0.9.
(a) Calculate the photon flux at the centre of the screen and the photocurrent in the detector.
(b) If the concave lens L of focal length 0.6 m is inserted in the aperture as shown, find the new values of photon flux and photocurrent. Assume a uniform average transmission of 80% from the lens.
(c) If the work function of the photoemissive surface is 1 eV, calculate the values of the stopping potential in the two cases (without and with the lens in the aperture).`,fig:{w:300,h:120,alt:"A point source S, a concave lens L in an aperture A at 0.6 m from S, and a screen SC 6 m beyond the lens with a detector D at its centre.",d:[["c",34,56,3,"F"],["t",34,44,"S","m"],["l",34,56,270,56,"d"],["p","M86 24Q94 56 86 88M96 24Q88 56 96 88"],["t",91,18,"L","m"],["t",91,106,"aperture A","ms"],["l",270,20,270,92,"B"],["t",270,14,"SC","m"],["r",266,50,8,12,"F"],["t",284,60,"D"],["l",34,112,86,112,"b"],["t",60,108,"0.6 m","ms"],["l",96,112,270,112,"b"],["t",183,108,"6 m","ms"]]},a:"(a) photon flux \u2248 1.1 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 per m\xB2 per s; photocurrent \u2248 7.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 A (b) flux \u2248 2.4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075 per m\xB2 per s; photocurrent \u2248 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 A (c) stopping potential \u2248 1.07 V in both cases",s:`Photon energy = 1240/600 = 2.07 eV, so photons per second from 2 W: N = 2/(2.07 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 6.0\xD710\xB9\u2078.
(a) The detector is 0.6 + 6 = 6.6 m from S: flux = N/(4\u03C0 \xD7 6.6\xB2) = 1.1\xD710\xB9\u2076 per m\xB2 per s. Photoelectrons per second = 0.9 \xD7 1.1\xD710\xB9\u2076 \xD7 0.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 = 4.96\xD710\xB9\xB9, so the current is 4.96\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 7.9\xD710\u207B\u2078 A.
(b) Photons through the aperture: N \xD7 (\u03C0 \xD7 0.05\xB2)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 0.6\xB2) = 1.05\xD710\xB9\u2076 per s; 80 % get through the lens. The concave lens (f = \u22120.6 m, object 0.6 m) gives a virtual source 0.3 m behind it, so the beam spreads to a radius 0.05 \xD7 (6.3/0.3) = 1.05 m on the screen. Flux = 0.8 \xD7 1.05\xD710\xB9\u2076/(\u03C0 \xD7 1.05\xB2) = 2.4\xD710\xB9\u2075 per m\xB2 per s and the current is 1.7\xD710\u207B\u2078 A.
(c) V\u2080 = 2.07 \u2212 1 = 1.07 V, the same in both cases (it does not depend on the intensity).
Note: the printed answers are 2.07 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 A, 1.483 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 A and 1.06 V; our working gives different currents. The work function is partly hidden by a watermark and is read as 1 eV (the printed 1.06 V agrees). Please check the currents with a teacher.`},{pn:127,y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"photo",st:"Threshold and work function",q:"A beam of light has three wavelengths 4144 \xC5, 4972 \xC5 and 6216 \xC5 with a total intensity of 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 W m\u207B\xB2 equally distributed amongst the three wavelengths. The beam falls normally on an area 1.0 cm\xB2 of a clean metallic surface of work function 2.3 eV. Assume that there is no loss of light by reflection and that each energetically capable photon ejects one electron. Calculate the number of photoelectrons liberated in two seconds.",s:`Photon energies (1240/\u03BB in nm): 2.99 eV, 2.49 eV, 1.99 eV. Only the first two exceed 2.3 eV.
Power in each wavelength = (1.2\xD710\u207B\xB3 W/m\xB2)(10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2) = 1.2\xD710\u207B\u2077 W.
Photons per second: 1.2\xD710\u207B\u2077/(2.99\xD71.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 2.5\xD710\xB9\xB9 and 1.2\xD710\u207B\u2077/(2.49\xD71.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 3.0\xD710\xB9\xB9.
Total \u2248 5.5\xD710\xB9\xB9 per second, so in 2 s: about 1.1\xD710\xB9\xB2 electrons.`,a:"about 1.1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2 photoelectrons"},{pn:128,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"True or False: In a photoelectric emission process the maximum energy of the photoelectrons increases with increasing intensity of the incident light.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Kmax = h\u03C5 \u2212 \u03C6 depends only on the frequency and the metal.
Intensity changes only the number of photoelectrons (the saturation current), not their maximum energy.
The statement is false.`},{pn:129,y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"photo",st:"Observations and true-or-false",q:"True or False: The kinetic energy of photoelectrons emitted by a photosensitive surface depends on the intensity of the incident radiation.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency and work function only.
Intensity affects only how many electrons are emitted per second. The statement is false.
Note: the crop prints this in the True or False section after a Fill in the Blanks heading, and gives no options; it is treated here as true or false.`},{pn:130,y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"eq",st:"Graphs: Kmax\u2013f and V\u2080\u2013f",q:"Fill in the blank: The maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted in the photoelectric effect is linearly dependent on the ______ of the incident radiation.",s:"Kmax = hf \u2212 \u03C6 is a straight line in the frequency f, with slope h.",a:"frequency"},{pn:131,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron with mass 'm' with an initial velocity v\u20D7 = v\u2080 \xEE (v\u2080 > 0) enters a magnetic field B\u20D7 = B\u2080 \u0135. If the initial de-Broglie wavelength at t = 0 is \u03BB\u2080, then its value after time 't' would be",o:["\u03BB\u2080 / \u221A(1 + e\xB2B\u2080\xB2t\xB2/m\xB2)","\u03BB\u2080","\u03BB\u2080 / \u221A(1 \u2212 e\xB2B\u2080\xB2t\xB2/m\xB2)","\u03BB\u2080 \u221A(1 + e\xB2B\u2080\xB2t\xB2/m\xB2)"],c:1,s:`The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work.
The speed, hence the momentum magnitude, stays constant.
\u03BB = h/p does not change: \u03BB\u2080.`},{pn:132,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"eq",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"A photoemissive substance is illuminated with a radiation of wavelength \u03BB\u1D62 so that it releases electrons with de-Broglie wavelength \u03BB\u2091. The longest wavelength of radiation that can emit photoelectron is \u03BB\u2080. Expression for de-Broglie wavelength is given by (m: mass of the electron, h: Planck's constant and c: speed of light)",o:["\u03BB\u2091 = \u221A(h\u03BB\u2080 / 2mc)","\u03BB\u2091 = \u221A( h / (2mc (1/\u03BB\u1D62 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2080)) )","\u03BB\u2091 = \u221A(h\u03BB\u1D62 / 2mc)","\u03BB\u2091 = h / \u221A( 2mc (1/\u03BB\u1D62 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2080) )"],c:1,s:`Kmax = hc(1/\u03BB\u1D62 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2080).
\u03BB\u2091 = h/\u221A(2m Kmax), so \u03BB\u2091\xB2 = h\xB2/(2m\xB7hc(1/\u03BB\u1D62 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2080)) = h/(2mc(1/\u03BB\u1D62 \u2212 1/\u03BB\u2080)).
Taking the square root gives option (b) (the whole fraction is under the root).`},{pn:133,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"A sub-atomic particle of mass 10\u207B\xB3\u2070 kg is moving with a velocity 2.21 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s. Under the matter wave consideration, the particle will behave closely like ______. (h = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J\xB7s)",o:["Infra-red radiation","Gamma rays","X-rays","Visible radiation"],c:2,s:`\u03BB = h/(mv) = 6.63\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074/(10\u207B\xB3\u2070 \xD7 2.21\xD710\u2076) = 3\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 m = 3 \xC5.
This wavelength lies in the X-ray range.`},{pn:134,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron of mass 'm' with an initial velocity v\u20D7 = v\u2080 \xEE (v\u2080 > 0) enters an electric field E\u20D7 = \u2212E\u2080 k\u0302. If the initial de-Broglie wavelength is \u03BB\u2080, the value after time t would be",o:["\u03BB\u2080 \u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u03BB\u2080 / \u221A(1 \u2212 e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u03BB\u2080 / \u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u03BB\u2080"],c:2,s:`The field is along z, so the x-momentum stays mv\u2080 and a z-momentum eE\u2080t builds up.
p\xB2 = m\xB2v\u2080\xB2 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2 = m\xB2v\u2080\xB2(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2)).
\u03BB = h/p = \u03BB\u2080/\u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2)).`},{pn:135,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton of mass 'm_p' has same energy as that of photon of wavelength '\u03BB'. If the proton is moving at non-relativistic speed, then ratio of its de Broglie wavelength to the wavelength of photon is",o:["(1/c)\u221A(E/(2m_p))","(1/c)\u221A(2E/m_p)","(1/c)\u221A(E/m_p)","(1/2c)\u221A(E/m_p)"],c:0,s:`Photon energy E = hc/\u03BB, so \u03BB = hc/E.
Proton: \u03BBp = h/\u221A(2m_pE).
\u03BBp/\u03BB = [h/\u221A(2m_pE)] \xD7 [E/(hc)] = \u221AE/(c\u221A(2m_p)) = (1/c)\u221A(E/(2m_p)).`},{pn:136,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"If \u03BB and K are de-Broglie wavelength and kinetic energy, respectively, of a particle with constant mass. The correct graphical representation for the particle will be (graphs of 1/K against \u03BB)",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Four graphs of 1/K against wavelength: (a) curve rising from the origin and flattening, (b) falling hyperbola-like curve, (c) curve rising from the origin and getting steeper, (d) straight line through the origin",d:[["t",12,18,"(a)","s"],["l",36,95,148,95,"a"],["l",36,95,36,24,"a"],["t",148,107,"\u03BB","es"],["t",40,26,"1/K","s"],["p","M36 95 C50 50 90 34 140 30"],["t",182,18,"(b)","s"],["l",206,95,318,95,"a"],["l",206,95,206,24,"a"],["t",318,107,"\u03BB","es"],["t",210,26,"1/K","s"],["p","M214 30 C222 70 250 88 312 92"],["t",12,126,"(c)","s"],["l",36,205,148,205,"a"],["l",36,205,36,134,"a"],["t",148,217,"\u03BB","es"],["t",40,142,"1/K","s"],["p","M36 205 C80 203 110 190 128 140"],["t",182,126,"(d)","s"],["l",206,205,318,205,"a"],["l",206,205,206,134,"a"],["t",318,217,"\u03BB","es"],["t",210,142,"1/K","s"],["l",206,205,300,145]]},o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:2,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK), so \u03BB\xB2 = h\xB2/(2mK) and 1/K = 2m\u03BB\xB2/h\xB2.
1/K is proportional to \u03BB\xB2: a parabola through the origin that rises ever more steeply.
That is graph (c).`},{pn:137,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton and an electron are associated with same de-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of their kinetic energies is (Assume h = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 Js, m\u2091 = 9.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg and m_p = 1836 times m\u2091)",o:["1 : 1836","1 : 1/1836","1 : \u221A1836","1 : 1/\u221A1836"],c:0,s:`Same \u03BB means same momentum p.
K = p\xB2/(2m), so K_p/K_e = m\u2091/m_p = 1/1836.
K_p : K_e = 1 : 1836.
Note: no key is printed; working gives (a).`},{pn:138,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton and an electron have the same de Broglie wavelength. If K_p and K_e be the kinetic energies of proton and electron respectively, then choose the correct relation.",o:["K_p = K_e","K_p > K_e","K_p = K_e\xB2","K_p < K_e"],c:3,s:`Same \u03BB gives same p, and K = p\xB2/(2m).
The proton is heavier, so its kinetic energy is smaller: K_p < K_e.`},{pn:139,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton, an electron and an alpha particle have the same energies. Their de-Broglie wavelengths will be compared as",o:["\u03BB\u2091 > \u03BB_\u03B1 > \u03BB_p","\u03BB_p > \u03BB\u2091 > \u03BB_\u03B1","\u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091 < \u03BB_\u03B1"],c:2,s:`For equal K, \u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK) \u221D 1/\u221Am.
Masses: electron < proton < alpha.
So \u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091.`},{pn:140,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength of a proton and an \u03B1-particle are \u03BB and 2\u03BB respectively. The ratio of the velocities of proton and \u03B1-particle will be",o:["1 : 8","4 : 1","8 : 1","1 : 2"],c:2,s:`v = h/(m\u03BB).
v_p/v_\u03B1 = (m_\u03B1\u03BB_\u03B1)/(m_p\u03BB_p) = (4 \xD7 2\u03BB)/(1 \xD7 \u03BB) = 8.
Ratio 8 : 1.`},{pn:141,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron is the same as that of a photon. If velocity of electron is 25% of the velocity of light, then the ratio of K.E. of electron and K.E. of photon will be",o:["1/8","1/1","8/1","1/4"],c:0,s:`Same \u03BB means same momentum p.
Electron: K = \xBDpv = \xBDp(c/4) = pc/8.
Photon: K = pc.
Ratio = 1/8.
Note: the exam tag is not printed beside this question on the crop; a faint line above it reads like 'Main 29th Jan 1st Shift 2024'.`},{pn:142,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The kinetic energy of an electron, \u03B1-particle and a proton are given as 4 K, 2 K and K respectively. The de-Broglie wavelength associated with electron (\u03BB\u2091), \u03B1-particle (\u03BB_\u03B1) and the proton (\u03BB_p) are as follows",o:["\u03BB_\u03B1 = \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 = \u03BB_p > \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 > \u03BB_p > \u03BB\u2091"],c:2,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK).
Proton: 2m_pK. Alpha: 2(4m_p)(2K) = 16 m_pK, which is 8 times the proton value, so \u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p.
Electron: 2m\u2091(4K) = 8m\u2091K, far smaller than 2m_pK because m\u2091 is tiny, so \u03BB\u2091 is the largest.
\u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091.`},{pn:143,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"Proton (P) and electron (e) will have same de-Broglie wavelength when the ratio of their momentum is (assume, m_p = 1849 m\u2091)",o:["1 : 43","43 : 1","1 : 1849","1 : 1"],c:3,s:"\u03BB = h/p. Equal wavelengths mean equal momenta, so the ratio is 1 : 1 whatever the masses."},{pn:144,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength of a molecule in a gas at room temperature (300 K) is \u03BB\u2081. If the temperature of the gas is increased to 600 K, then the de-Broglie wavelength of the same gas molecule becomes",o:["2\u03BB\u2081","\u221A2 \u03BB\u2081","\xBD \u03BB\u2081","(1/\u221A2) \u03BB\u2081"],c:3,s:`Thermal kinetic energy \u221D T, so p = \u221A(2mK) \u221D \u221AT.
\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AT.
T doubles, so \u03BB becomes \u03BB\u2081/\u221A2.`},{pn:145,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths of proton and electron having same kinetic energy (Assume m_p = m\u2091 \xD7 1849)",o:["1 : 62","1 : 43","2 : 43","1 : 30"],c:1,s:`For equal K, \u03BB \u221D 1/\u221Am.
\u03BB_p/\u03BB\u2091 = \u221A(m\u2091/m_p) = 1/\u221A1849 = 1/43.`},{pn:146,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"A proton and an \u03B1-particle are accelerated from rest by 2 V and 4 V potentials respectively. The ratio of their de-Broglie wavelength is",o:["16 : 1","8 : 1","2 : 1","4 : 1"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV).
Proton: m\xB7e\xB72 = 2me. Alpha: (4m)(2e)(4) = 32me.
\u03BB_p/\u03BB_\u03B1 = \u221A(32/2) = 4.
Ratio 4 : 1.`},{pn:147,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron having kinetic energy E is \u03BB. If the kinetic energy of electron becomes E/4, then its de-Broglie wavelength will be",o:["\u221A2 \u03BB","2\u03BB","\u03BB/\u221A2","\u03BB/2"],c:1,s:`\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AE.
E becomes E/4, so \u03BB becomes \u03BB \xD7 \u221A4 = 2\u03BB.`},{pn:148,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An \u03B1-particle, a proton and an electron have the same kinetic energy. Which one of the following is correct in case of their de-Broglie wavelength?",o:["\u03BB_\u03B1 > \u03BB_p > \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 < \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 = \u03BB_p = \u03BB\u2091","\u03BB_\u03B1 > \u03BB_p < \u03BB\u2091"],c:1,s:`For equal K, \u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK) \u221D 1/\u221Am.
The heavier the particle, the shorter its wavelength: alpha < proton < electron.`},{pn:149,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of an \u03B1 particle and a proton accelerated from rest by the same potential is 1/\u221Am, the value of m is",o:["4","16","2","8"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV) \u221D 1/\u221A(mq).
\u03BB_\u03B1/\u03BB_p = \u221A(m_pq_p/(m_\u03B1q_\u03B1)) = \u221A(1/(4 \xD7 2)) = 1/\u221A8.
So m = 8.`},{pn:150,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron accelerated through a potential difference V\u2081 has a de-Broglie wavelength of \u03BB. When the potential is changed to V\u2082, its de-Broglie wavelength increases by 50%. The value of (V\u2081/V\u2082) is equal to",o:["4","9/4","3","3/2"],c:1,s:`\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AV, so V \u221D 1/\u03BB\xB2.
New wavelength = 1.5\u03BB.
V\u2081/V\u2082 = (\u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081)\xB2 = 1.5\xB2 = 9/4.
Note: no key is printed for this question; working gives (b).`},{pn:151,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The ratio of wavelengths of proton and deuteron accelerated by potentials Vp and Vd is 1 : \u221A2. Then the ratio of Vp to Vd will be",o:["1 : 1","\u221A2 : 1","2 : 1","4 : 1"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV), so \u03BB\xB2 \u221D 1/(mV) (same charge).
(\u03BBp/\u03BBd)\xB2 = md\xB7Vd/(mp\xB7Vp) = 2Vd/Vp = 1/2.
Vp/Vd = 4.`},{pn:152,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A nucleus of mass M at rest splits into two parts having masses M\u2032/3 and 2M\u2032/3 (M\u2032 < M). The ratio of de-Broglie wavelengths of the two parts will be",o:["1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 1","2 : 3"],c:2,s:`Momentum is conserved, starting from rest, so the two parts have equal and opposite momenta.
\u03BB = h/p is the same for both, ratio 1 : 1.`},{pn:153,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron (mass m) with an initial velocity v\u20D7 = v\u2080 \xEE (v\u2080 > 0) is moving in an electric field E\u20D7 = \u2212E\u2080 \xEE (E\u2080 > 0), where E\u2080 is constant. If at t = 0 the de-Broglie wavelength is \u03BB\u2080 = h/(mv\u2080), then its de-Broglie wavelength after time t is given by",o:["\u03BB\u2080","\u03BB\u2080(1 + eE\u2080t/(mv\u2080))","\u03BB\u2080t","\u03BB\u2080/(1 + eE\u2080t/(mv\u2080))"],c:3,s:`Force on the electron = (\u2212e)(\u2212E\u2080 \xEE) = +eE\u2080 \xEE, so it speeds up.
v = v\u2080 + eE\u2080t/m.
\u03BB = h/(mv) = h/(mv\u2080(1 + eE\u2080t/(mv\u2080))) = \u03BB\u2080/(1 + eE\u2080t/(mv\u2080)).`},{pn:154,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:`The equation \u03BB = 1.227/x nm can be used to find the de-Broglie wavelength of an electron. In this equation x stands for
where m = mass of electron, P = momentum of electron, K = kinetic energy of electron, V = accelerating potential in volts for electron`,o:["\u221A(mK)","\u221AP","\u221AK","\u221AV"],c:3,s:`For an electron accelerated through V volts, \u03BB = h/\u221A(2meV) = 1.227/\u221AV nm.
So x = \u221AV.`},{pn:155,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An \u03B1 particle and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference. The ratio of linear momenta acquired by the above two particles will be",o:["\u221A2 : 1","2\u221A2 : 1","4\u221A2 : 1","8 : 1"],c:1,s:`p = \u221A(2mqV).
p\u03B1/pp = \u221A((4m\xB72e)/(m\xB7e)) = \u221A8 = 2\u221A2.
Ratio 2\u221A2 : 1.`},{pn:156,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton, a neutron, an electron and an \u03B1-particle have the same energy. If \u03BBp, \u03BBn, \u03BBe and \u03BB\u03B1 are the de-Broglie wavelengths of proton, neutron, electron and \u03B1-particle respectively, then choose the correct relation from the following",o:["\u03BBp = \u03BBn > \u03BBe > \u03BB\u03B1","\u03BB\u03B1 < \u03BBn < \u03BBp < \u03BBe","\u03BBe < \u03BBp = \u03BBn > \u03BB\u03B1","\u03BBe = \u03BBp = \u03BBn = \u03BB\u03B1"],c:1,s:`For equal energy, \u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK) \u221D 1/\u221Am.
Mass order: electron < proton < neutron < \u03B1.
So \u03BBe > \u03BBp > \u03BBn > \u03BB\u03B1, i.e. \u03BB\u03B1 < \u03BBn < \u03BBp < \u03BBe.`},{pn:157,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron with speed v and a photon with speed c have the same de-Broglie wavelength. If the kinetic energy and momentum of the electron are Ee and pe and that of the photon are Eph and pph respectively, which of the following is correct?",o:["Ee/Eph = 2c/v","Ee/Eph = v/(2c)","pe/pph = 2c/v","pe/pph = v/(2c)"],c:1,s:`Same \u03BB means same momentum: pe = pph = p, so options (c), (d) fail.
Ee = \xBDpv, Eph = pc.
Ee/Eph = v/(2c).`},{pn:158,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An \u03B1 particle and a carbon-12 atom have the same kinetic energy K. The ratio of their de-Broglie wavelengths (\u03BB\u03B1 : \u03BBC12) is",o:["1 : \u221A3","\u221A3 : 1","3 : 1","2 : \u221A3"],c:1,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK) \u221D 1/\u221Am.
m\u03B1 = 4 u, mC = 12 u.
\u03BB\u03B1/\u03BBC = \u221A(12/4) = \u221A3, so \u221A3 : 1.`},{pn:159,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The de-Broglie wavelengths for an electron and a photon are \u03BBe and \u03BBp respectively. For the same kinetic energy of electron and photon, which of the following presents the correct relation between the de-Broglie wavelengths of the two?",o:["\u03BBp \u221D \u03BBe\xB2","\u03BBp \u221D \u03BBe","\u03BBp \u221D \u221A\u03BBe","\u03BBp \u221D \u221A(1/\u03BBe)"],c:0,s:`Electron: K = h\xB2/(2m\u03BBe\xB2).
Photon: K = hc/\u03BBp, so \u03BBp = hc/K = 2mc\u03BBe\xB2/h.
Therefore \u03BBp \u221D \u03BBe\xB2.`},{pn:160,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength of a particle having kinetic energy E is \u03BB. How much extra energy must be given to this particle so that the de-Broglie wavelength reduces to 75% of the initial value?",o:["E","7E/9","16E/9","E/9"],c:1,s:`\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AK. New \u03BB = 0.75\u03BB, so \u221A(K\u2032) = \u221AE/0.75.
K\u2032 = E/0.5625 = 16E/9.
Extra energy = 16E/9 \u2212 E = 7E/9.`},{pn:161,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A moving proton and electron have the same de-Broglie wavelength. If K and P denote the K.E. and momentum respectively, then choose the correct option.",o:["Kp > Ke and Pp = Pe","Kp < Ke and Pp = Pe","Kp < Ke and Pp < Pe","Kp = Ke and Pp = Pe"],c:1,s:`Same \u03BB gives the same momentum, Pp = Pe.
K = P\xB2/2m, and the proton is heavier, so Kp < Ke.`},{pn:162,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The temperature of an ideal gas in 3-dimensions is 300 K. The corresponding de-Broglie wavelength of the electron approximately at 300 K is [me = mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, h = Planck constant = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, kB = Boltzmann constant = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J K\u207B\xB9]",o:["2.26 nm","3.25 nm","8.46 nm","6.26 nm"],c:3,s:`Mean thermal energy (3/2)kT = p\xB2/2m, so p = \u221A(3mkT).
3mkT = 3 \xD7 9e-31 \xD7 1.38e-23 \xD7 300 = 1.118e-50, p = 1.057e-25.
\u03BB = 6.6e-34/1.057e-25 = 6.24e-9 m, about 6.26 nm.`},{pn:163,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"With what speed should a galaxy move outward, with respect to earth, so that the sodium-D line at wavelength 5890 \xC5 is observed at 5896 \xC5?",o:["296 km/sec","322 km/sec","336 km/sec","306 km/sec"],c:3,s:`Note: no key is printed on the crop; working gives 306 km/s. This is a Doppler-shift question (not a de Broglie one).
v = c\xB7\u0394\u03BB/\u03BB = 3e5 km/s \xD7 6/5890 = 305.6 km/s \u2248 306 km/s.`},{pn:164,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron of mass me and a proton of mass mp are accelerated through the same potential difference. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron to that with the proton is",o:["1","mp/me","me/mp","\u221A(mp/me)"],c:3,s:`Same charge and same V give the same K, so \u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK) \u221D 1/\u221Am.
\u03BBe/\u03BBp = \u221A(mp/me).`},{pn:165,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle of mass 4M at rest disintegrates into two particles of mass M and 3M respectively having non zero velocities. The ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of particle of mass M to that of 3M will be",o:["1 : 3","1 : \u221A3","1 : 1","3 : 1"],c:2,s:`Starting at rest, the two fragments have equal and opposite momenta.
\u03BB = h/p is therefore equal, ratio 1 : 1.`},{pn:166,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron moving with speed v and a photon moving with speed c have the same de-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of kinetic energy of electron to that of photon is",o:["v/(3c)","3c/v","2c/v","v/(2c)"],c:3,s:`Same \u03BB means the same momentum p.
Ke = \xBDpv, Kph = pc.
Ke/Kph = v/(2c).`},{pn:167,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength associated with an electron and a proton were calculated by accelerating them through the same potential of 100 V. What should be the ratio of their wavelengths? (mp = 1.00727 u, me = 0.00055 u)",o:["1860 : 1","41.4 : 1","43 : 1","(1860)\xB2 : 1"],c:2,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV) \u221D 1/\u221Am for the same q and V.
\u03BBe/\u03BBp = \u221A(1.00727/0.00055) = \u221A1831 = 42.8, about 43 : 1.`},{pn:168,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron of mass m and a photon have the same energy E. The ratio of wavelength of electron to that of photon is (c being the velocity of light)",o:["c(2mE)^(1/2)","(E/2m)^(1/2)","(1/c)(2m/E)^(1/2)","(1/c)(E/2m)^(1/2)"],c:3,s:`Electron: \u03BBe = h/\u221A(2mE).
Photon: \u03BBph = hc/E.
\u03BBe/\u03BBph = E/(c\u221A(2mE)) = (1/c)\u221A(E/2m).`},{pn:169,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle is travelling 4 times as fast as an electron. Assuming the ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of a particle to that of electron is 2 : 1, the mass of the particle is",o:["16 times the mass of e\u207B","1/16 times the mass of e\u207B","8 times the mass of e\u207B","1/8 times the mass of e\u207B"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/(mv), so \u03BBp/\u03BBe = (me\xB7ve)/(mp\xB7vp) = me/(4mp) = 2.
mp = me/8.`},{pn:170,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The de Broglie wavelength of a proton and \u03B1-particle are equal. The ratio of their velocities is",o:["4 : 3","4 : 2","4 : 1","1 : 4"],c:2,s:`mp\xB7vp = m\u03B1\xB7v\u03B1, so vp/v\u03B1 = m\u03B1/mp = 4/1.
Ratio 4 : 1.`},{pn:171,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An \u03B1-particle and a proton are accelerated from rest by a potential difference of 200 V. After this, their de-Broglie wavelengths are \u03BB\u03B1 and \u03BBp respectively. The ratio \u03BBp/\u03BB\u03B1 is",o:["8","3.8","7.8","2.8"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV) \u221D 1/\u221A(mq).
\u03BBp/\u03BB\u03B1 = \u221A((m\u03B1 q\u03B1)/(mp qp)) = \u221A(4 \xD7 2) = \u221A8 = 2.83, about 2.8.`},{pn:172,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron of mass me and a proton of mass Mp = 1836 me are moving with the same speed. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelength \u03BBelectron/\u03BBproton will be",o:["1","1/1836","918","1836"],c:3,s:`\u03BB = h/(mv) \u221D 1/m at equal speed.
\u03BBe/\u03BBp = Mp/me = 1836.`},{pn:173,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle is moving 5 times as fast as an electron. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelength of the particle to that of the electron is 1.878 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074. The mass of the particle is close to",o:["9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg","9.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2078 kg","4.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg","1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2078 kg"],c:1,s:`\u03BBp/\u03BBe = (me\xB7ve)/(mp\xB7vp) = me/(5mp) = 1.878e-4.
mp = 9.1e-31/(5 \xD7 1.878e-4) = 9.1e-31/9.39e-4 = 9.7e-28 kg.`},{pn:174,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"Particle A of mass mA = m/2 moving along the x-axis with velocity v\u2080 collides elastically with another particle B at rest having mass mB = m/3. If both particles move along the x-axis after the collision, the change \u0394\u03BB in de-Broglie wavelength of particle A, in terms of its de-Broglie wavelength (\u03BB\u2080) before collision, is",o:["\u0394\u03BB = 2\u03BB\u2080","\u0394\u03BB = (3/2)\u03BB\u2080","\u0394\u03BB = (5/2)\u03BB\u2080","\u0394\u03BB = 4\u03BB\u2080"],c:3,s:`Elastic collision with B at rest: vA\u2032 = (mA \u2212 mB)/(mA + mB)\xB7v\u2080 = (1/6)/(5/6)\xB7v\u2080 = v\u2080/5.
\u03BB = h/(mA v), so \u03BB\u2032 = 5\u03BB\u2080.
\u0394\u03BB = 5\u03BB\u2080 \u2212 \u03BB\u2080 = 4\u03BB\u2080.`},{pn:175,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron, a doubly ionized helium ion (He\u207A\u207A) and a proton are having the same kinetic energy. The relation between their respective de-Broglie wavelengths \u03BBe, \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A and \u03BBp is",o:["\u03BBe > \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A = \u03BBp","\u03BBe < \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A = \u03BBp","\u03BBe > \u03BBp > \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A","\u03BBe < \u03BBp < \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A"],c:2,s:`Same K gives \u03BB \u221D 1/\u221Am.
Mass order: electron < proton < He\u207A\u207A (4 u), so \u03BBe > \u03BBp > \u03BBHe\u207A\u207A.`},{pn:176,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"Assuming the nitrogen molecule is moving with r.m.s. velocity at 400 K, the de-Broglie wavelength of nitrogen molecule is close to (Given: nitrogen molecule weight: 4.64 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg, Boltzmann constant: 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J/K, Planck's constant: 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["0.24 \xC5","0.20 \xC5","0.34 \xC5","0.44 \xC5"],c:0,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3kT/m), so \u03BB = h/(m v) = h/\u221A(3mkT).
3mkT = 3 \xD7 4.64e-26 \xD7 1.38e-23 \xD7 400 = 7.68e-46, root = 2.77e-23.
\u03BB = 6.63e-34/2.77e-23 = 2.39e-11 m = 0.24 \xC5.`},{pn:177,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"An electron (of mass m) and a photon have the same energy E in the range of a few eV. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron and the wavelength of the photon is (c = speed of light in vacuum)",o:["(E/2m)^(1/2)","c(2mE)^(1/2)","(1/c)(2E/m)^(1/2)","(1/c)(E/2m)^(1/2)"],c:3,s:`Electron: \u03BBe = h/\u221A(2mE). Photon: \u03BB = hc/E.
Ratio = E/(c\u221A(2mE)) = (1/c)\u221A(E/2m).`},{pn:178,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Kmax and stopping potential",q:"When a photon of energy 4.0 eV strikes the surface of a metal A, the ejected photoelectrons have maximum kinetic energy TA eV and de-Broglie wavelength \u03BBA. The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons liberated from another metal B by a photon of energy 4.50 eV is TB = (TA \u2212 1.5) eV. If the de-Broglie wavelength of these photoelectrons is \u03BBB = 2\u03BBA, then the work function of metal B is",o:["1.5 eV","4 eV","3 eV","2 eV"],c:1,s:`\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AT, so \u03BBB = 2\u03BBA means TB = TA/4.
TA/4 = TA \u2212 1.5 gives TA = 2 eV, TB = 0.5 eV.
\u03C6B = 4.5 \u2212 0.5 = 4 eV.`},{pn:179,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron (mass m) with initial velocity v\u20D7 = v\u2080 \xEE + v\u2080 \u0135 is in an electric field E\u20D7 = \u2212E\u2080 k\u0302. If \u03BB\u2080 is the initial de-Broglie wavelength of the electron, its de-Broglie wavelength at time t is given by",o:["\u03BB\u2080/\u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u03BB\u2080/\u221A(2 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u03BB\u2080/\u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(2m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))","\u221A2 \u03BB\u2080/\u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(m\xB2v\u2080\xB2))"],c:2,s:`Initial speed = \u221A2 v\u2080, so \u03BB\u2080 = h/(m\u221A2 v\u2080).
Force on electron is +eE\u2080 k\u0302, so vz = eE\u2080t/m. Speed\xB2 = 2v\u2080\xB2 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/m\xB2.
\u03BB/\u03BB\u2080 = \u221A2 v\u2080/\u221A(2v\u2080\xB2 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/m\xB2) = 1/\u221A(1 + e\xB2E\u2080\xB2t\xB2/(2m\xB2v\u2080\xB2)).`},{pn:180,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle moving with kinetic energy E has de Broglie wavelength \u03BB. If energy \u0394E is added to its energy, the wavelength becomes \u03BB/2. Value of \u0394E is",o:["2E","4E","3E","E"],c:2,s:`\u03BB \u221D 1/\u221AK, so halving \u03BB needs K\u2032 = 4E.
\u0394E = 4E \u2212 E = 3E.`},{pn:181,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron of mass m and magnitude of charge |e| initially at rest gets accelerated by a constant electric field E. The rate of change of de-Broglie wavelength of this electron at time t, ignoring relativistic effects, is",o:["\u2212h/(|e|Et\xB2)","h/(|e|Et)","\u2212h/(|e|Et)","h/(|e|E\u221At)"],c:0,s:`v = eEt/m, so p = eEt and \u03BB = h/(eEt).
d\u03BB/dt = \u2212h/(eEt\xB2).`},{pn:182,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"Two particles move at right angle to each other. Their de Broglie wavelengths are \u03BB\u2081 and \u03BB\u2082 respectively. The particles suffer perfectly inelastic collision. The de Broglie wavelength \u03BB of the final particle is given by",o:["2/\u03BB = 1/\u03BB\u2081 + 1/\u03BB\u2082","\u03BB = \u221A(\u03BB\u2081\u03BB\u2082)","1/\u03BB\xB2 = 1/\u03BB\u2081\xB2 + 1/\u03BB\u2082\xB2","\u03BB = (\u03BB\u2081 + \u03BB\u2082)/2"],c:2,s:`Momentum is conserved as a vector; the two momenta are perpendicular.
p\xB2 = p\u2081\xB2 + p\u2082\xB2, and p = h/\u03BB.
So 1/\u03BB\xB2 = 1/\u03BB\u2081\xB2 + 1/\u03BB\u2082\xB2.`},{pn:183,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A nucleus A, with a finite de-Broglie wavelength \u03BB_A, undergoes spontaneous fission into two nuclei B and C of equal mass. B flies in the same direction as that of A, while C flies in the opposite direction with a velocity equal to half of that of B. The de-Broglie wavelengths \u03BB_B and \u03BB_C of B and C are respectively",o:["\u03BB_A, \u03BB_A/2","2\u03BB_A, \u03BB_A","\u03BB_A, 2\u03BB_A","\u03BB_A/2, \u03BB_A"],c:3,s:`Let A have mass M, velocity v, so p = Mv. B, C have mass M/2; vB = u, vC = u/2 backwards.
Mv = (M/2)u \u2212 (M/2)(u/2) = Mu/4, so u = 4v.
pB = (M/2)(4v) = 2p, so \u03BB_B = \u03BB_A/2. pC = (M/2)(2v) = p, so \u03BB_C = \u03BB_A.`},{pn:184,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle 'P' is formed due to a completely inelastic collision of particles 'x' and 'y' having de-Broglie wavelengths '\u03BB_x' and '\u03BB_y' respectively. If x and y were moving in opposite directions, then the de-Broglie wavelength of 'P' is",o:["\u03BB_x\u03BB_y/(\u03BB_x + \u03BB_y)","\u03BB_x \u2212 \u03BB_y","\u03BB_x + \u03BB_y","\u03BB_x\u03BB_y/|\u03BB_x \u2212 \u03BB_y|"],c:3,s:`Opposite directions: p = |p_x \u2212 p_y| = h|1/\u03BB_x \u2212 1/\u03BB_y|.
\u03BB = h/p = \u03BB_x\u03BB_y/|\u03BB_y \u2212 \u03BB_x|.`},{pn:185,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"In an electron microscope, the resolution that can be achieved is of the order of the wavelength of electrons used. To resolve a width of 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 m, the minimum electron energy required is close to",o:["25 keV","100 keV","1 keV","500 keV"],c:0,s:`\u03BB = 0.075 \xC5. For electrons \u03BB(\xC5) = 12.27/\u221AV, so V = (12.27/0.075)\xB2 \u2248 2.7 \xD7 10\u2074 V.
Energy \u2248 27 keV, closest to 25 keV.`},{pn:186,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"If the de Broglie wavelength of an electron is equal to 10\u207B\xB3 times the wavelength of a photon of frequency 6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074 Hz, then the speed of electron is equal to (Speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s, Planck's constant = 6.63 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s and Mass of electron = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",o:["1.7 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","1.8 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","1.45 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","1.1 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s"],c:2,s:`Photon \u03BB = c/f = 3\xD710\u2078/6\xD710\xB9\u2074 = 5\xD710\u207B\u2077 m. Electron \u03BB = 5\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070 m.
v = h/(m\u03BB) = 6.63\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074/(9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070) \u2248 1.46\xD710\u2076 m/s.`},{pn:187,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"A particle A of mass m and charge q is accelerated by a potential difference of 50 V. Another particle B of mass 4m and charge q is accelerated by a potential difference of 2500 V. The ratio of de-Broglie wavelengths \u03BB_A/\u03BB_B is close to",o:["14.14","0.07","4.47","10.00"],c:0,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV).
\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = \u221A(4m\xB72500)/\u221A(m\xB750) = \u221A200 \u2248 14.14.`},{pn:188,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"Two electrons are moving with non-relativistic speeds perpendicular to each other. If corresponding de Broglie wavelengths are \u03BB\u2081 and \u03BB\u2082, their de Broglie wavelength in the frame of reference attached to their centre of mass is",o:["\u03BB_CM = 2\u03BB\u2081\u03BB\u2082/\u221A(\u03BB\u2081\xB2 + \u03BB\u2082\xB2)","\u03BB_CM = \u03BB\u2081 = \u03BB\u2082","\u03BB_CM = (\u03BB\u2081 + \u03BB\u2082)/2","1/\u03BB_CM = 1/\u03BB\u2081 + 1/\u03BB\u2082"],c:0,s:`Equal masses, so in the CM frame each electron has velocity (v\u2081 \u2212 v\u2082)/2 (vector).
Speed = \u221A(v\u2081\xB2 + v\u2082\xB2)/2, with v = h/(m\u03BB).
\u03BB_CM = 2h/(m\u221A(v\u2081\xB2+v\u2082\xB2)) = 2\u03BB\u2081\u03BB\u2082/\u221A(\u03BB\u2081\xB2 + \u03BB\u2082\xB2).`},{pn:189,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"If the de-Broglie wavelengths associated with a proton and an \u03B1-particle are equal, then the ratio of velocities of the proton and the \u03B1-particle will be",o:["1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 4","4 : 1"],c:3,s:`Equal \u03BB means equal momentum: m_p v_p = m_\u03B1 v_\u03B1.
v_p/v_\u03B1 = m_\u03B1/m_p = 4, so 4 : 1.`},{pn:190,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"Bohr orbit as standing wave",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength (\u03BB_B) associated with the electron orbiting in the second excited state of hydrogen atom is related to that in the ground state (\u03BB_G) by",o:["\u03BB_B = \u03BB_G/3","\u03BB_B = 3\u03BB_G","\u03BB_B = \u03BB_G/2","\u03BB_B = 2\u03BB_G"],c:1,s:`Second excited state is n = 3.
Standing wave: 2\u03C0r = n\u03BB and r \u221D n\xB2 give \u03BB \u221D n.
So \u03BB_B = 3\u03BB_G.`},{pn:191,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle A of mass m and initial velocity v collides with a particle B of mass m/2 which is at rest. The collision is head on, and elastic. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths \u03BB_A to \u03BB_B after the collision is",o:["\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = 1/3","\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = 2","\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = 2/3","\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = 1/2"],c:1,s:`Elastic head-on: v_A = (m \u2212 m/2)v/(m + m/2) = v/3; v_B = 2m v/(1.5m) = 4v/3.
p_A = mv/3, p_B = (m/2)(4v/3) = 2mv/3.
\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = p_B/p_A = 2.`},{pn:192,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"de-Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated by a voltage of 50 V is close to (|e| = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, m_e = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, h = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["0.5 \xC5","1.2 \xC5","1.7 \xC5","2.4 \xC5"],c:2,s:"\u03BB = h/\u221A(2meV) = 12.27/\u221A50 \xC5 \u2248 1.7 \xC5."},{pn:193,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"Bohr orbit as standing wave",q:"The de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron in the n = 4 level is",o:["two times the de-Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state","four times the de-Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state","half of the de-Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state","1/4th of the de-Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state"],c:1,s:`2\u03C0r = n\u03BB with r \u221D n\xB2, so \u03BB \u221D n.
n = 4 gives 4 times the ground-state wavelength.`},{pn:194,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A parallel beam of electrons travelling in x-direction falls on a slit of width d (see figure). If after passing the slit, an electron acquires momentum p_y in the y-direction then for a majority of electrons passing through the slit (h is Planck's constant)",o:["|p_y|d \u2248 h","|p_y|d > h","|p_y|d < h","|p_y|d >> h"],c:2,s:`Most electrons land in the central diffraction maximum, whose edge is at sin\u03B8 = \u03BB/d.
There p_y = p sin\u03B8 \u2264 h/d.
So for the majority |p_y|d < h.`,fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Parallel electron beam moving along x toward a slit of width d in a vertical barrier; x and y axes shown",d:[["l",150,15,150,60,"B"],["l",150,90,150,135,"B"],["l",20,25,146,25,"a"],["l",20,40,146,40,"a"],["l",20,55,146,55,"a"],["l",20,75,146,75,"a"],["l",20,95,146,95,"a"],["l",20,110,146,110,"a"],["l",20,125,146,125,"a"],["l",165,60,165,90,"b"],["t",172,79,"d"],["l",210,75,280,75,"a"],["t",284,79,"x"],["l",210,75,210,25,"a"],["t",216,28,"y"]]}},{pn:195,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"If the kinetic energy of a free electron doubles, its de-Broglie wavelength changes by the factor",o:["1/\u221A2","\u221A2","1/2","2"],c:0,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK), so \u03BB \u221D K^(\u22121/2).
Doubling K multiplies \u03BB by 1/\u221A2.`},{pn:196,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A beam of electrons is used in a Young's double slit experiment. The slit width is d. When the velocity of electron is increased, then",o:["no interference is observed","fringe width increases","fringe width decreases","fringe width remains same"],c:2,s:`Fringe width \u03B2 = \u03BBD/d.
Higher speed means larger p and smaller \u03BB = h/p, so the fringe width decreases.
Note: the year is read from a blurred print.`},{pn:197,y:"IIT-JEE (year not printed)",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A proton has kinetic energy E = 100 keV which is equal to that of a photon. The wavelength of photon is \u03BB\u2082 and that of proton is \u03BB\u2081. The ratio \u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 is proportional to",o:["E\xB2","E^(1/2)","E^(\u22121)","E^(\u22121/2)"],c:3,s:`Proton: \u03BB\u2081 = h/\u221A(2mE). Photon: \u03BB\u2082 = hc/E.
\u03BB\u2082/\u03BB\u2081 = c\u221A(2mE)/E \u221D E^(\u22121/2).`},{pn:198,y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle of mass M at rest decays into two particles of masses m\u2081 and m\u2082, having nonzero velocities. The ratio of the de Broglie wavelengths of the particles, \u03BB\u2081/\u03BB\u2082, is",o:["m\u2081/m\u2082","m\u2082/m\u2081","1.0","\u221Am\u2082/\u221Am\u2081"],c:2,s:`Initial momentum is zero, so the fragments have equal and opposite momenta.
\u03BB = h/p is equal for both, ratio 1.0.`},{pn:199,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"eq",st:"Electron at the anode and charging of a plate",q:"Light of wavelength \u03BB_ph falls on a cathode plate inside a vacuum tube as shown in the figure. The work function of the cathode surface is \u03C6 and the anode is a wire mesh of conducting material kept at a distance d from the cathode. A potential difference V is maintained between the electrodes. If the minimum de Broglie wavelength of the electrons passing through the anode is \u03BB_e, which of the following statement(s) is(are) true?",o:["For large potential difference (V >> \u03C6/e), \u03BB_e is approximately halved if V is made four times","\u03BB_e decreases with increase in \u03C6 and \u03BB_ph","\u03BB_e increases at the same rate as \u03BB_ph for \u03BB_ph < hc/\u03C6","\u03BB_e is approximately halved, if d is doubled"],c:0,s:`Fastest electrons at the anode have K = hc/\u03BB_ph \u2212 \u03C6 + eV, and \u03BB_e = h/\u221A(2mK).
(a) For V large, K \u2248 eV, \u03BB_e \u221D V^(\u22121/2): four times V halves \u03BB_e. True.
(b) Larger \u03C6 lowers K, so \u03BB_e increases. False; (c) \u03BB_e is not proportional to \u03BB_ph. False.
(d) K depends on V, not on d. False.`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Vacuum tube with a cathode plate on the left lit by light, a wire-mesh anode on the right, electrons moving between them, and a battery connected across the plates",d:[["r",40,35,240,90],["l",70,45,70,115,"B"],["l",240,45,240,115,"B","d"],["l",90,60,225,60,"a"],["l",90,80,225,80,"a"],["l",90,100,225,100,"a"],["l",25,15,64,52,"a"],["t",8,12,"Light"],["t",244,40,"Electrons","s"],["l",70,115,70,150],["l",70,150,125,150],["l",125,140,125,160],["l",135,146,135,154,"B"],["l",135,150,240,150],["l",240,150,240,115],["t",95,168,"V"],["l",70,128,240,128,"bd"],["t",150,126,"d","m"]]}},{pn:200,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"eq",st:"Two lights on one metal",q:"When photons of energy 4.25 eV strike the surface of metal A, the ejected photoelectrons have maximum kinetic energy T_A eV and de Broglie wavelength \u03BB_A. The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons liberated from another metal B by photons of energy 4.70 eV is T_B = (T_A \u2212 1.50) eV. If the de Broglie wavelength of these photoelectrons is \u03BB_B = 2\u03BB_A, then",o:["the work function of A is 2.25 eV","the work function of B is 4.20 eV","T_A = 2.00 eV","T_B = 2.75 eV"],c:[0,1,2],s:`\u03BB_B = 2\u03BB_A means p_B = p_A/2, so T_B = T_A/4.
T_A/4 = T_A \u2212 1.50 gives T_A = 2.00 eV, T_B = 0.50 eV.
\u03C6_A = 4.25 \u2212 2.00 = 2.25 eV; \u03C6_B = 4.70 \u2212 0.50 = 4.20 eV.
So (a), (b), (c) are correct; (d) is wrong. No printed key for this one.`},{pn:201,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An electron is released from rest near an infinite non-conducting sheet of uniform charge density \u2212\u03C3. The rate of change of de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron varies inversely as nth power of time. The numerical value of n is ______.",n:2,s:`The field of the sheet is uniform, so acceleration is constant: v = at.
\u03BB = h/(mat) \u221D t\u207B\xB9, so d\u03BB/dt \u221D t\u207B\xB2.
n = 2.`},{pn:202,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"A particle of mass 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg travels in a medium with a speed of 10\u2076 m/s and a photon of a radiation of linear momentum 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg m/s travels in vacuum. The wavelength of photon is ______ times the wavelength of the particle.",n:910,s:`\u03BB = h/p for both.
\u03BB_photon/\u03BB_particle = p_particle/p_photon = (9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 10\u2076)/10\u207B\xB2\u2077 = 910.`},{pn:203,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"A proton is fired from very far away towards a nucleus with charge Q = 120e, where e is the electronic charge. It makes a closest approach of 10 fm to the nucleus. The de Broglie wavelength (in units of fm) of the proton at its start is ______. (Take the proton mass, m_p = (5/3) \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg; h/e = 4.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 J s/C; 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 m/F; 1 fm = 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 m)",n:7,s:`At closest approach all KE becomes potential energy: K = (9\xD710\u2079)(120e)(e)/10\u207B\xB9\u2074 = 2.76\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2 J (about 17.3 MeV).
p = \u221A(2mK) = \u221A(2 \xD7 1.667\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 2.765\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB2) \u2248 9.6\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2070.
h = 4.2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 6.72\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074, so \u03BB = 7\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2075 m = 7 fm.`},{pn:204,y:"IIT-JEE (year not printed)",tp:"matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength and voltage",q:"An \u03B1-particle and a proton are accelerated from rest by a potential difference of 100 V. After this, their de Broglie wavelengths are \u03BB_\u03B1 and \u03BB_p respectively. The ratio \u03BB_p/\u03BB_\u03B1, to the nearest integer, is ______.",n:3,s:`\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mqV).
\u03BB_p/\u03BB_\u03B1 = \u221A(m_\u03B1 q_\u03B1/(m_p q_p)) = \u221A(4 \xD7 2) = \u221A8 \u2248 2.83.
Nearest integer: 3.`},{pn:205,y:"IIT-JEE (year not printed)",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:`Paragraph: Wave property of electrons implies that they will show diffraction effects. Davisson and Germer demonstrated this by diffracting electrons from crystals. The law governing the diffraction from a crystal is obtained by requiring that electron waves reflected from the planes of atoms in a crystal interfere constructively (see figure).

Electrons accelerated by potential V are diffracted from a crystal. If d = 1 \xC5 and i = 30\xB0, V should be about (h = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s, m_e = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)`,o:["1000 V","2000 V","50 V","500 V"],c:2,s:`Constructive reflection (first order): 2d cos i = \u03BB, so \u03BB = 2 \xD7 1 \xD7 cos30\xB0 = 1.73 \xC5.
V = h\xB2/(2me\u03BB\xB2) = (6.6\xD710\u207B\xB3\u2074)\xB2/(2 \xD7 9.1\xD710\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 3\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2070) \u2248 50 V.`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"Electron ray arriving at a crystal plane at angle i from the normal and reflecting off the top plane, with equally spaced parallel planes a distance d apart",d:[["l",20,100,280,100],["l",20,130,280,130],["l",20,160,280,160],["l",40,30,140,100,"a"],["l",140,100,240,30,"a"],["l",140,40,140,100,"d"],["t",148,70,"i"],["l",260,100,260,130,"b"],["t",266,119,"d"],["t",20,22,"incoming electrons","s"],["t",190,22,"outgoing","s"]]}},{pn:206,y:"IIT-JEE (year not printed)",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:"If a strong diffraction peak is observed when electrons are incident at an angle i from the normal to the crystal planes with distance d between them (see figure), de Broglie wavelength \u03BB_dB of electrons can be calculated by the relationship (n is an integer)",o:["d cos i = n\u03BB_dB","d sin i = n\u03BB_dB","2d cos i = n\u03BB_dB","2d sin i = n\u03BB_dB"],c:2,s:`With i measured from the normal, the glancing angle is 90\xB0 \u2212 i.
Path difference between adjacent planes = 2d cos i.
Constructive: 2d cos i = n\u03BB_dB.`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"Electron ray arriving at a crystal plane at angle i from the normal and reflecting off the top plane, with equally spaced parallel planes a distance d apart",d:[["l",20,100,280,100],["l",20,130,280,130],["l",20,160,280,160],["l",40,30,140,100,"a"],["l",140,100,240,30,"a"],["l",140,40,140,100,"d"],["t",148,70,"i"],["l",260,100,260,130,"b"],["t",266,119,"d"]]}},{pn:207,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:"In an experiment, electrons are made to pass through a narrow slit of width d comparable to their de Broglie wavelength. They are detected on a screen at a distance D from the slit. Which of the following graphs can be expected to represent the number of electrons N detected as a function of the detector position y (y = 0 corresponds to the middle of the slit)? In each graph y is vertical and N increases to the left; the dashed bar marked d shows the slit width.",o:["(a) a smooth bell-shaped curve, wider than the slit, with a single central peak at y = 0","(b) a narrow sharp peak lying inside the slit width","(c) a flat rectangle exactly as wide as the slit","(d) a flat-topped dome exactly as wide as the slit"],c:0,s:`Slit width comparable to \u03BB gives strong diffraction: the central maximum is broad and spreads beyond the slit width, falling smoothly.
Not a sharp geometric shadow ((c), (d)) and not a narrow spike ((b)).
So (a).`,fig:{w:420,h:215,alt:"Electron beam through a slit and a screen at y = 0, followed by four candidate graphs (a) to (d) of electron number against position y",d:[["l",10,18,55,18,"a"],["l",10,28,55,28,"a"],["l",10,38,55,38,"a"],["l",10,48,55,48,"a"],["l",10,58,55,58,"a"],["l",65,12,65,30,"B"],["l",65,46,65,64,"B"],["l",150,10,150,68,"d"],["t",158,40,"y = 0","s"],["l",72,30,72,46,"b"],["t",78,41,"d","s"],["l",70,95,70,185,"a"],["l",75,125,75,155,"b"],["t",60,205,"(a)"],["l",170,95,170,185,"a"],["l",175,125,175,155,"b"],["t",160,205,"(b)"],["l",270,95,270,185,"a"],["l",275,125,275,155,"b"],["t",260,205,"(c)"],["l",370,95,370,185,"a"],["l",375,125,375,155,"b"],["t",360,205,"(d)"],["p","M70 100 C68 125 42 122 40 140 C42 158 68 155 70 180"],["p","M170 128 L140 140 L170 152"],["r",235,125,35,30],["p","M370 125 C325 125 325 155 370 155"]]}},{pn:208,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): The beam of electrons show wave nature and exhibit interference and diffraction. Reason (R): Davisson Germer experimentally verified the wave nature of electrons. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.",o:["(A) is correct but (R) is not correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct"],c:2,s:`Electrons do show interference and diffraction (A true).
Davisson\u2013Germer diffraction from a nickel crystal confirmed this (R true) and is the evidence for A.`},{pn:209,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Davisson-Germer experiment establishes the wave nature of electrons. Statement II: If electrons have wave nature, they can interfere and show diffraction. In the light of the above statements choose the correct answer from the option given below",o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:0,s:`Davisson\u2013Germer electron diffraction proved the wave nature: I is true.
Waves can interfere and diffract: II is true.`},{pn:210,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"Given below are two statements: Statement I: Two photons having equal linear momenta have equal wavelengths. Statement II: If the wavelength of photon is decreased, then the momentum and energy of a photon will also decrease. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.",o:["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],c:2,s:`p = h/\u03BB, so equal momenta mean equal wavelengths: I true.
Decreasing \u03BB raises both p = h/\u03BB and E = hc/\u03BB: II false.`},{pn:211,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:`Assertion A: An electron microscope can achieve better resolving power than an optical microscope.
Reason R: The de Broglie wavelength of the electrons emitted from an electron gun is much less than the wavelength of visible light.
Choose the correct answer.`,o:["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A."],c:3,s:`Resolving power is better for a smaller wavelength.
Accelerated electrons have \u03BB of about 0.01 to 0.1 nm, far below visible light (400-700 nm).
So A is true, and R is exactly why. Option (d).`},{pn:212,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"matter",st:"Davisson\u2013Germer and Bragg",q:`This question has Statement 1 and Statement 2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.
Statement 1: Davisson-Germer experiment established the wave nature of electrons.
Statement 2: If electrons have wave nature, they can interfere and show diffraction.`,o:["Statement 1 is true, statement 2 is false.","Statement 1 is true, statement 2 is true, statement 2 is the correct explanation for statement 1.","Statement 1 is true, statement 2 is true, statement 2 is not the correct explanation of statement 1.","Statement 1 is false, statement 2 is true."],c:1,s:`Davisson and Germer saw electrons diffract from a nickel crystal, which showed their wave nature.
Interference and diffraction are exactly what waves do, so statement 2 is true and explains statement 1.
Option (b).`},{pn:213,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"matter",st:"Comparing particles and photon vs electron",q:"The potential energy of a particle of mass m is V(x) = E\u2080 for 0 \u2264 x \u2264 1 and V(x) = 0 for x > 1. \u03BB\u2081 and \u03BB\u2082 are the de Broglie wavelengths of the particle when 0 \u2264 x \u2264 1 and x > 1 respectively. If the total energy of the particle is 2E\u2080, find \u03BB\u2081/\u03BB\u2082.",n:1.414,s:`For 0 \u2264 x \u2264 1: K\u2081 = 2E\u2080 \u2212 E\u2080 = E\u2080.
For x > 1: K\u2082 = 2E\u2080.
\u03BB = h/\u221A(2mK), so \u03BB\u2081/\u03BB\u2082 = \u221A(K\u2082/K\u2081) = \u221A2 = 1.414.`},{pn:214,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"matter",st:"Bohr orbit as standing wave",q:"Assume that the de Broglie waves associated with an electron can form a standing wave between the atoms arranged in a one-dimensional array with nodes at each of the atomic sites. It is found that one such standing wave is formed if the distance d between the atoms of the array is 2 \xC5. A similar standing wave is again formed if d is increased to 2.5 \xC5 but not for any intermediate value of d. Find the energy of the electrons in electron volt (give this number) and the least value of d for which the standing wave of the type described above can form.",n:151,s:`Nodes at the atoms give d = n\u03BB/2. Consecutive allowed values 2 \xC5 and 2.5 \xC5 differ by \u03BB/2, so \u03BB/2 = 0.5 \xC5, \u03BB = 1 \xC5 (and n = 4 for d = 2 \xC5).
E = 12.27\xB2/\u03BB\xB2(\xC5) eV \u2248 150 eV (about 151 eV).
Least d is for n = 1: d = \u03BB/2 = 0.5 \xC5.
Answer asked here: energy \u2248 150 eV; least d = 0.5 \xC5.`},{pn:215,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"An electron having de Broglie wavelength \u03BB is incident on a target in an X-ray tube. The cut-off wavelength of the emitted X-ray is",o:["0","hc/mc","2m\xB2c\xB2\u03BB\xB2/h\xB2","2mc\u03BB\xB2/h"],c:3,s:`Electron KE = p\xB2/2m = h\xB2/(2m\u03BB\xB2).
Cut-off: all KE goes to one photon, hc/\u03BB\u2080 = h\xB2/(2m\u03BB\xB2).
\u03BB\u2080 = 2mc\u03BB\xB2/h. Option (d).`},{pn:216,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"An X-ray tube is operated at 1.24 million volt. The shortest wavelength of the produced photon will be",o:["10\u207B\xB9 nm","10\u207B\u2074 nm","10\u207B\xB2 nm","10\u207B\xB3 nm"],c:3,s:"\u03BBmin = 1240 eV nm / (1.24\xD710\u2076 eV) = 10\u207B\xB3 nm. Option (d)."},{pn:217,y:"JEE Main 2012",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"An electron beam is accelerated by a potential difference V to hit a metallic target to produce X-rays. It produces continuous as well as characteristic X-rays. If \u03BBmin is the smallest possible wavelength of X-ray in the spectrum, the variation of log \u03BBmin with log V is correctly represented in the graph (see figure): (a) falling line, (b) line rising from a positive intercept, (c) line rising from the log V axis, (d) horizontal line.",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:0,s:`\u03BBmin = hc/(eV), so log \u03BBmin = log(hc/e) \u2212 log V.
This is a straight line of slope \u22121: falling. Option (a).`,fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"Four small graphs of log \u03BBmin against log V: (a) a straight line falling, (b) a line rising from a positive intercept, (c) a line rising from the horizontal axis, (d) a horizontal line.",d:[["l",40,60,40,12,"a"],["l",40,60,110,60,"a"],["l",40,20,95,60,"B"],["t",10,36,"(a)","s"],["t",80,72,"log V","s"],["t",42,8,"log \u03BBmin","s"],["l",190,60,190,12,"a"],["l",190,60,260,60,"a"],["l",190,38,250,18,"B"],["t",160,36,"(b)","s"],["t",230,72,"log V","s"],["t",192,8,"log \u03BBmin","s"],["l",40,140,40,92,"a"],["l",40,140,110,140,"a"],["l",52,140,100,98,"B"],["t",10,116,"(c)","s"],["t",80,152,"log V","s"],["t",42,88,"log \u03BBmin","s"],["l",190,140,190,92,"a"],["l",190,140,260,140,"a"],["l",190,110,250,110,"B"],["t",160,116,"(d)","s"],["t",230,152,"log V","s"],["t",192,88,"log \u03BBmin","s"]]}},{pn:218,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"If \u03BBCu is the wavelength of K\u03B1 X-ray line of copper (atomic number 29) and \u03BBMo is the wavelength of the K\u03B1 X-ray line of molybdenum (atomic number 42), then the ratio \u03BBCu/\u03BBMo is close to",o:["1.99","2.14","0.50","0.48"],c:1,s:"Moseley: 1/\u03BB \u221D (Z \u2212 1)\xB2, so \u03BBCu/\u03BBMo = (41/28)\xB2 = 2.14. Option (b)."},{pn:219,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"Which one of the following statements is WRONG in the context of X-rays generated from an X-ray tube?",o:["Wavelength of characteristic X-rays decreases when the atomic number of the target increases","Cut-off wavelength of the continuous X-rays depends on the atomic number of the target","Intensity of the characteristic X-rays depends on the electrical power given to the X-ray tube","Cut-off wavelength of the continuous X-rays depends on the energy of the electrons in the X-ray tube"],c:1,s:`(a) True: Moseley, \u03BB \u221D 1/(Z\u22121)\xB2.
(b) False: cut-off \u03BB = hc/eV depends only on the tube voltage, not on the target.
(c) True: more power means more electrons and more intensity.
(d) True: cut-off depends on the electron energy.
The wrong one is (b).`},{pn:220,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"Electrons with de Broglie wavelength \u03BB fall on the target in an X-ray tube. The cut-off wavelength of the emitted X-rays is",o:["\u03BB\u2080 = 2mc\u03BB\xB2/h","\u03BB\u2080 = 2h/(mc)","\u03BB\u2080 = 2m\xB2c\xB2\u03BB\xB3/h\xB2","\u03BB\u2080 = \u03BB"],c:0,s:`KE = h\xB2/(2m\u03BB\xB2) = hc/\u03BB\u2080.
\u03BB\u2080 = 2mc\u03BB\xB2/h. Option (a).`},{pn:221,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"K\u03B1 wavelength emitted by an atom of atomic number Z = 11 is \u03BB. Find the atomic number for an atom that emits K\u03B1 radiation with wavelength 4\u03BB.",o:["Z = 6","Z = 4","Z = 11","Z = 44"],c:0,s:`1/\u03BB \u221D (Z\u22121)\xB2. Wavelength 4 times larger means (Z\u22121) is half: (Z\u22121) = 10/2 = 5.
Z = 6. Option (a).`},{pn:222,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"The potential difference applied to an X-ray tube is 5 kV and the current through it is 3.2 mA. Then the number of electrons striking the target per second is",o:["2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","5 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078","1 \xD7 10\xB9\u2077","4 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075"],c:0,s:"N = I/e = 3.2\xD710\u207B\xB3 / 1.6\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 2\xD710\xB9\u2076 per second. Option (a)."},{pn:223,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"The intensity of X-rays from a Coolidge tube is plotted against wavelength \u03BB as shown in the figure. The minimum wavelength found is \u03BBc and the wavelength of the K\u03B1 line is \u03BBK. As the accelerating voltage is increased",o:["\u03BBK \u2212 \u03BBc increases","\u03BBK \u2212 \u03BBc decreases","\u03BBK increases","\u03BBK decreases"],c:0,s:`\u03BBc = hc/eV decreases as V increases.
\u03BBK is fixed by the target atom and does not change.
So \u03BBK \u2212 \u03BBc increases. Option (a).`,fig:{w:260,h:140,alt:"Graph of X-ray intensity I against wavelength \u03BB: a continuous curve starting at the minimum wavelength \u03BBc, rising to a peak, with a sharp spike at \u03BBK.",d:[["l",30,115,30,15,"a"],["l",30,115,240,115,"a"],["t",14,22,"I","s"],["t",232,128,"\u03BB","s"],["p","M70 115 L72 70 L100 85 L120 60 L140 100 L150 20 L160 90 L190 115","B"],["l",70,115,70,120,""],["t",64,130,"\u03BBc","s"],["t",144,130,"\u03BBK","s"]]}},{pn:224,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"Electrons with energy 80 keV are incident on the tungsten target of an X-ray tube. K shell electrons of tungsten have 72.5 keV energy. X-rays emitted by the tube contain only",o:["a continuous X-ray spectrum (Bremsstrahlung) with a minimum wavelength of 0.155 \xC5","a continuous X-ray spectrum (Bremsstrahlung) with all wavelengths","the characteristic X-ray spectrum of tungsten","a continuous X-ray spectrum (Bremsstrahlung) with a minimum wavelength of 0.155 \xC5 and the characteristic X-ray spectrum of tungsten"],c:3,s:`Cut-off \u03BB = 12400 eV \xC5 / 80000 eV = 0.155 \xC5, so a continuous spectrum with this minimum is produced.
80 keV is more than 72.5 keV, so K-shell electrons can be knocked out and characteristic lines also appear.
Option (d).`},{pn:225,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"The K\u03B1 X-ray emission line of tungsten occurs at \u03BB = 0.021 nm. The energy difference between K and L levels in this atom is about",o:["0.51 MeV","1.2 MeV","59 keV","13.6 eV"],c:2,s:"\u0394E = hc/\u03BB = 1240 eV nm / 0.021 nm \u2248 5.9\xD710\u2074 eV = 59 keV. Option (c)."},{pn:226,y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"In an X-ray tube, electrons emitted from a filament (cathode) carrying current I hit a target (anode) at a distance d from the cathode. The target is kept at a potential V higher than the cathode resulting in emission of continuous and characteristic X-rays. If the filament current I is decreased to I/2, the potential difference V is increased to 2V, and the separation distance d is reduced to d/2, then (one or more options correct)",o:["the cut-off wavelength will reduce to half, and wavelengths of the characteristic X-rays will remain the same","the cut-off wavelength as well as the wavelengths of the characteristic X-rays will remain the same","the cut-off wavelength will reduce to half, and the intensities of all the X-rays will decrease","the cut-off wavelength will become two times larger, and the intensity of all the X-rays will decrease"],c:[0,2],s:`Cut-off \u03BB = hc/eV, so doubling V halves it.
Characteristic wavelengths depend only on the target atom, so they stay the same.
Half the filament current means fewer electrons, so intensities fall (the extra voltage does not make up for it in the intended answer).
Correct: (a) and (c).`},{pn:227,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube operating at a given accelerating voltage. The wavelength of the continuous X-rays has values from",o:["0 to \u221E","\u03BBmin to \u221E where \u03BBmin > 0","0 to \u03BBmax where \u03BBmax < \u221E","\u03BBmin to \u03BBmax where 0 < \u03BBmin < \u03BBmax < \u221E"],c:1,s:`Electrons lose energy in steps of any size up to eV, so photon energies range from near 0 up to eV.
Wavelengths therefore run from \u03BBmin = hc/eV (greater than 0) up to very large values (no upper limit in practice).
Option (b).`},{pn:228,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"The potential difference applied to an X-ray tube is increased. As a result, in the emitted radiation (one or more options correct)",o:["the intensity increases","the minimum wavelength increases","the intensity remains unchanged","the minimum wavelength decreases"],c:[0,3],s:`Note: the printed key says (c,d); working gives (a,d). (a) and (c) contradict each other, so (c,d) cannot both hold.
\u03BBmin = hc/eV decreases with V, so (d) is true.
Each electron carries more energy and emits more X-ray energy, so the total intensity rises, so (a) is true.`},{pn:229,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"The X-ray beam coming from an X-ray tube will be",o:["monochromatic","having all wavelengths smaller than a certain maximum wavelength","having all wavelengths larger than a certain minimum wavelength","having all wavelengths lying between a minimum and a maximum wavelength"],c:2,s:`The beam has a continuous spectrum plus characteristic lines.
There is a sharp minimum wavelength hc/eV and no maximum.
So it has all wavelengths larger than a certain minimum. Option (c).`},{pn:230,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"The shortest wavelength of X-rays emitted from an X-ray tube depends on (one or more options correct)",o:["the current in the tube","the voltage applied to the tube","the nature of the gas in the tube","the atomic number of the target material"],c:1,s:`Note: the printed key says (b,d); working gives (b).
\u03BBmin = hc/(eV) depends only on the applied voltage.
Current, gas and target atomic number do not enter.`},{pn:231,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"The wavelength of an X-ray beam is 10 \xC5. The mass of a fictitious particle having the same energy as that of the X-ray photons is (x/3)h kg. The value of x is ___. [h = Planck's constant]",n:10,s:`Set mc\xB2 = hc/\u03BB, so m = h/(c\u03BB).
m = h / (3\xD710\u2078 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079) = h/0.3 = (10/3)h.
So x = 10.`},{pn:232,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:`Statement-1: If the accelerating potential in an X-ray tube is increased, the wavelengths of the characteristic X-rays do not change.
Statement-2: When an electron beam strikes the target in an X-ray tube, part of the kinetic energy is converted into X-ray energy.`,o:["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1.","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1.","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false.","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],c:1,s:`Characteristic wavelengths depend only on the target atom's energy levels: statement 1 true.
Statement 2 is also true, but it explains the continuous X-rays, not why characteristic lines stay fixed.
Option (b).`},{pn:233,y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"A potential difference of 20 kV is applied across an X-ray tube. The minimum wavelength of X-rays generated is ___ \xC5.",n:.62,s:"\u03BBmin = 12400 eV \xC5 / 20000 eV = 0.62 \xC5."},{pn:234,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"photon",st:"Energy, momentum and number of photons",q:"In an X-ray tube, electrons accelerated through a potential difference of 15,000 volt strike a copper target. The speed of the emitted X-rays inside the tube is ___ m/s.",n:3e8,s:"X-rays are electromagnetic waves, so in vacuum their speed is c = 3\xD710\u2078 m/s whatever the voltage."},{pn:235,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"photon",st:"X-ray cutoff wavelength",q:"Fill in the blanks: When the number of electrons striking the anode of an X-ray tube is increased, the ______ of the emitted X-rays increases, while when the speed of the electrons striking the anode is increased, the cut-off wavelength of the emitted X-rays ______.",a:"intensity; decreases",s:`More electrons per second give more X-ray photons per second, so the intensity increases.
A greater speed means a larger accelerating voltage, so the greatest photon energy eV is larger and \u03BBmin = hc/eV becomes smaller: the cut-off wavelength decreases.
Note: the key printed in the book reads \u201CFrequency, decreases\u201D; the number of electrons does not change the frequency limit (it depends on the speed only), so the first blank is intensity.`}]})();
